:title("Kotlin (subset) to LLVM IR compiler") ;
:description("Compiles Kotlin to an LLVM IR module and runs it with the built-in
IR interpreter and the MetaJS handle runtime (llvm.RunJS).

The language: top level and local functions with block or expression bodies, DEFAULT
parameter values, NAMED arguments, vararg parameters and the spread operator f(*arr);
val/var declarations with type inference, if/else and when as statements and as
expressions (subject, val-binding subject, comma separated values, range and is-type
branches), the elvis operator, safe calls x?.length / obj?.m(), while, do-while,
for over ranges and lists, break/continue, labelled loops with break@l / continue@l,
labelled and non-local returns out of a lambda, try/catch/finally as a statement AND as
an expression (the value is the winning clause's last expression), throw as a statement
and as an expression (Nothing), lambdas (destructuring parameters included) and
anonymous functions as values, lambdas WITH RECEIVER (val f: Int.(Int) -> Int), trailing
lambda call syntax, the list higher order methods map/filter/sumOf/forEach/count/any,
string templates and raw strings, listOf() and the typed arrayOf family, the stdlib
infix operators (a to b builds a Pair, also readable via component1/component2;
and/or/xor; shl/shr/ushr) and USER declared infix functions (a dot b is a.dot(b)),
Int.inv(), the builtins println/print/abs/max/min and exitProcess.

Types: classes with primary constructor properties, SECONDARY CONSTRUCTORS (dispatched
by argument count), init blocks and property initializers run in declaration order,
property ACCESSORS with a backing field (get()/set(v) and `field`), open/abstract classes
and single INHERITANCE with super.m() calls, INTERFACES with default methods and
interface DELEGATION (: I by impl), DATA and VALUE classes (componentN, equals, toString,
copy with named arguments), ENUM classes (entries with constructor arguments, name,
ordinal, members, bare entry names inside them), sealed hierarchies, objects and
companion objects (named ones reachable as Cls.Name), object expressions, NESTED and
INNER classes (this@Outer reads the stored outer receiver), GENERICS (erased, with the
where clause) including REIFIED type parameters (x is T works, the type argument travels
as a hidden parameter), destructuring declarations / for-loops / lambda parameters via
the componentN convention, typealiases and annotation classes (both erased), and
Kotlin's OPERATOR CONVENTIONS: plus/minus/times/div/rem, unaryMinus, get, invoke,
contains and equals - each lowered with a runtime class probe, so a program that declares
no convention method emits exactly the IR it emitted before.

Extensions: top level and local extension FUNCTIONS with a body and extension
PROPERTIES (val Recv.name get() = ...). Dispatch is by NAME (the receiver type is
dropped), so an extension must not reuse a builtin or class member name - those win.

Delegated properties: `val x by lazy { init }` is genuine (the initializer runs once and
its value becomes a plain field; eager, so it is computed at the declaration, not on
first access); every other delegate (by inject(), custom getValue/setValue) is not in
the subset.

Known gaps and deviations. Char is a character CODE, but the runtime's string indexing
yields a one-character string, so 'a' + 1 == 'b' works while \"abc\"[1] == 'b' does not,
and a Char put into a string renders as its number (a char literal interpolated directly,
\"${'$'}\", is folded at compile time and is exact). An unqualified name inside an
extension body resolves through js_ktget, which falls back to the properties of `this`
only when the receiver is an object - so a bare `length` on a String receiver fails
(write this.length). A range VALUE is materialized as a list (Kotlin's progression is
lazy). A non-local return is delivered by raising a marker object, so a user
catch(e: Exception) between the lambda and its function would see it. componentN on a
value that has none yields undefined instead of being rejected. Type arguments are
erased, so variance, bounds and overload resolution by type are not enforced; a method
name declared by two classes shares one named-argument slot list. Not in the subset:
reflection, member-level extension functions, non-lazy property delegates.

COROUTINES run SYNCHRONOUSLY. runBlocking, coroutineScope, supervisorScope and
withContext are their bodies; async and launch run their block at the point it is
written and answer a Job whose await/join are immediate; delay and yield() are no-ops;
Dispatchers.IO and friends are names that carry no behaviour. So the RESULTS are right
and the ORDERING is not: nothing suspends, so two coroutines never interleave and a
program whose output depends on interleaving prints a different (but deterministic)
order than kotlinx.coroutines. Flow, Channel, select and cancellation are not
modelled. `suspend` itself remains syntax only.

A SEQUENCE IS EAGER: sequenceOf is listOf and every operator on it is the list
operator, so a pipeline over a finite source gives Kotlin's answer while an INFINITE
generateSequence is materialised up to a cap of 100000 elements (the cap prints a
warning when it is reached) rather than being pulled lazily. take() on an
infinite generator therefore still answers correctly; a generator with side effects
runs them 100000 times, and one that needs more than 100000 elements is truncated.
The one LAZY sequence is the sequence { } builder's: its block runs on a coroutine
of its own and yield(v) suspends it, so a for loop or an iterator() interleaves with
the block as in Kotlin, map / mapNotNull / filter / filterNot / take / drop /
takeWhile / dropWhile / onEach answer another lazy sequence, and first / firstOrNull /
find pull only what they need. Every other member materializes it, with the same
100000-element cap. yield() with no argument is the coroutine no-op above.

A SET is its own shape - it deduplicates, compares without regard to order and is
never equal to a List - while an ARRAY still collapses onto a list. hashCode() is
java's throughout: the 31-fold for a list, the SUM for a set, doubleToLongBits for a
Double. A qualified stdlib path (kotlin.math.abs) resolves to the same builtin as the
bare name.

REGULAR EXPRESSIONS are genuine, over the shared engine (abnf/jsrtregex.go plus the
Kotlin dispatcher abnf/jsrtregexkt.go, reached through the js_rxkt externals; the
interpreter grammar runs the same engine as languages/lib/regex.js). Kotlin has no
regexp literal, so a pattern arrives as Regex(p), Regex(p, options) or p.toRegex(options);
the options are RegexOption entries, singly or in a setOf(...). IGNORE_CASE,
DOT_MATCHES_ALL, MULTILINE and COMMENTS map onto the engine's neutral i / s / m / x,
LITERAL quotes the pattern instead of being a mode, and CANON_EQ and UNIX_LINES are
REPORTED as unsupported rather than silently ignored. Regex answers matches (whole
input), containsMatchIn, find, findAll, matchEntire, replace (with a string template or
a lambda), replaceFirst, split with an optional limit, pattern and toString;
Regex.escape and Regex.escapeReplacement are the quoting helpers. A MatchResult carries
value, range, groupValues, groups (by number and by name), destructured for
val (a, b) = m.destructured, and next(). String.matches, .replace, .split and .contains
take a Regex too, and only then - a String argument keeps the plain behavior. The
replacement template is Kotlin's own dollar-one / dollar-brace-name spelling, rewritten
before emission to what the engine expands. The engine has no lookbehind, no POSIX
bracket expressions and no unicode property escapes.

Functions, methods and lambdas become IR functions jsf_N(env, args) (methods get the
instance prepended, lambdas close over their scope via js_closure), classes become
descriptor objects linked by __super (the chain the runtime walks for method lookup,
super calls and `is`), accessors are js_defprop pairs on the descriptor, implicit
property access goes through the js_ktget/js_kset externals (locals first, then the
properties of this), method calls dispatch through js_mcall (which also implements the
list higher order methods), and ?. compiles to a null test branching around the access
(a phi merges the null). The whole program becomes jsmain(env, args).
The entry point is fun main(); self checking programs end with exitProcess(failures).
The same language with the same semantics is interpreted directly by kotlin-interpreter.abnf.") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib). lib/cstyle-comments.abnf is NOT
// included: Kotlin's whitespace set is wider than C's, so the same four rules
// are spelled out below with the extra separators. Everything else is shared.
:include("lib/ident.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// Kotlin's whitespace, a local copy of lib/cstyle-comments.abnf with ONE extra
// blank: the form feed / page break (x0c). What belongs here is settled by the
// expected TOKEN DUMPS in testData/lexer/kotlin/whitespaceCharacters, and they
// are unambiguous - pageBreak.txt records WHITE_SPACE, while verticalTab.txt,
// nextLine.txt, lineSeparator.txt and paragraphSeparator.txt all record
// BAD_CHARACTER. The vertical tab (x0b), NEL (x85), LINE SEPARATOR (x2028) and
// PARAGRAPH SEPARATOR (x2029) are therefore characters Kotlin REFUSES between
// tokens; accepting them only wins files kotlinc itself rejects.
// Inside a string or char literal the vertical tab and the form feed are both
// ordinary content (verticalTabInStringLiterals and pageBreakInStringLiterals
// record REGULAR_STRING_PART), which is what TplPlain and CharPlain allow.
// The form feed stays Kotlin-local rather than going into the shared fragment,
// which about fifteen other grammars include.
// A line comment still ends at a LINE FEED only, which is what the Kotlin lexer
// does too, so NotNewline is unchanged.
Whitespace  = { @+" \t\r\n\x0c" | Comment } ;
Comment     = LineComment | BlockComment ;
LineComment = "//" :whitespace() { NotNewline } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// Kotlin block comments NEST (psi/NestedComments.kt): /*/**/*/ is one comment.
BlockComment = KDocOk "/*" :whitespace() { NestedComment | NotStar | "*" { "*" } NotStarSlash } "*" { "*" } "/" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// A KDoc comment is NOT trivia to Kotlin's parser. Twin of KDocOk in
// kotlin-interpreter.abnf; see there.
KDocOk      = :script(~~
    (function() {
        if (c.peek(0) !== 47 || c.peek(1) !== 42 || c.peek(2) !== 42) { return undefined }
        var tags = ["see", "throws", "exception", "sample", "param", "property"]
        var i = 3
        var steps = 0
        var bad = false
        while (steps < 20000) {
            steps++
            var ch = c.peek(i)
            if (ch < 0) { break }
            if (ch === 42 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) { break }
            if (ch === 91) {
                // A bracket run, up to the closing `]` on the SAME line.
                var j = i + 1
                var body = ""
                var n = 0
                var cc = c.peek(j)
                while (cc >= 0 && cc !== 93 && cc !== 10 && n < 256) {
                    body = body + String.fromCharCode(cc)
                    j++
                    n++
                    cc = c.peek(j)
                }
                if (cc === 93 && body.length > 0) {
                    var f = body.charCodeAt(0)
                    var isName = (f >= 97 && f <= 122) || (f >= 65 && f <= 90) || f === 95 || f === 96
                    var spaced = body.indexOf(" ") >= 0 || body.indexOf("\t") >= 0
                    if (isName && !spaced && body.charCodeAt(body.length - 1) === 46) { bad = true }
                }
                if (bad) { break }
                i = j
                continue
            }
            if (ch === 64) {
                // A tag. Only the ones whose subject KDoc parses as a NAME are checked.
                var k = i + 1
                var w = ""
                var wn = 0
                var wc = c.peek(k)
                while (((wc >= 97 && wc <= 122) || (wc >= 65 && wc <= 90)) && wn < 24) {
                    w = w + String.fromCharCode(wc)
                    k++
                    wn++
                    wc = c.peek(k)
                }
                var known = false
                var t = 0
                while (t < tags.length) {
                    if (tags[t] === w) { known = true }
                    t++
                }
                if (known) {
                    while (c.peek(k) === 32 || c.peek(k) === 9) { k++ }
                    var name = ""
                    var nn = 0
                    var nc = c.peek(k)
                    while (nn < 256 && ((nc >= 97 && nc <= 122) || (nc >= 65 && nc <= 90) || (nc >= 48 && nc <= 57) || nc === 95 || nc === 46 || nc === 96)) {
                        name = name + String.fromCharCode(nc)
                        k++
                        nn++
                        nc = c.peek(k)
                    }
                    if (name.length > 0 && name.charCodeAt(name.length - 1) === 46) { bad = true; break }
                }
                i = k
                continue
            }
            i++
        }
        if (bad) { return abnf.newToken("\x01kdoc-dangling-name", 0) }
    })()
~~) ;
NestedComment = "/*" { NestedComment | NotStar | "*" { "*" } NotStarSlash } "*" { "*" } "/" ;
NotNewline  = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b"..."\U0010ffff" ;
NotStar     = "\x00"..."\x29" | "\x2b"..."\U0010ffff" ;
NotStarSlash = "\x00"..."\x29" | "\x2b"..."\x2e" | "\x30"..."\U0010ffff" ;

Program     <~~ buildMain(takeAll()) ~~>
            = { Annotation } [ [ Modifiers ] Package ] { Import } { TopItem } { Annotation } ;
// One top-level item. Factored out of Program (the alternatives and their ORDER are
// unchanged) so that each one is numbered as it is walked: buildMain emits the class
// items in topological order rather than source order, and it needs to know which
// slots they occupy. noteTopItem and ClassMark are item-neutral - each pops the thunk
// the alternative pushed and pushes it straight back.
TopItem     <~~ push(noteTopItem(pop())) ~~>
            = [ Modifiers ] ( TypeMark | FunDecl <~~ push(makeFunStmt(pop())) ~~> | ExtFun | UnsupFun | ClassMark | ExtProp | ExtDelegProp | TopAccProp | UnsupAccessor | TopProp ) ;
TypeMark    <~~ push(markTopType(pop())) ~~>
            = UnsupType ;
ClassMark   <~~ push(markTopType(pop())) ~~>
            = ClassDecl ;

// Modifiers and annotations before a declaration are parsed and ignored (they do
// not change what we lower). Annotations accept ALL forms: an argument list
// @Suppress("Unused") / @Deprecated("m", ReplaceWith("n")), qualified names
// @a.b.C, use-site targets @field:A / @get:A / @file:A, and annotation arrays
// @[A B C(x)]. Modifiers that would change the shape of the body
// (enum/annotation/companion) are NOT consumed here - those declaration forms
// route to notImplemented instead.
Modifiers   = { Modifier | Annotation | AnnEnumMod | VarianceMod } ;
// `annotation` and `enum` as ordinary modifiers, kept off `annotation class` /
// `enum class` by NotClassW. Twin of AnnEnumMod in kotlin-interpreter.abnf; see there.
AnnEnumMod  = ( "annotation" | "enum" ) KwEnd NotColon NotClassW ;
// `in` and `out` are variance modifiers, and Kotlin lets them stand in an ordinary
// declaration modifier run as well. They are the two modifier words that are also HARD
// operators - `a in b`, `for (x in xs)`, `in 1..2 ->` - so they are only taken when a
// modifier, a declaration keyword or an annotation follows.
VarianceMod = ( "in" | "out" ) KwEnd ModWordAhead ;
// A zero-width guard that fails unless the next token starts a modifier run or a
// declaration - an `@` annotation, or one of the modifier / declaration keywords.
ModWordAhead = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var kws = "|public|private|protected|internal|open|final|abstract|sealed|data"
                + "|override|lateinit|const|inner|vararg|noinline|crossinline|tailrec"
                + "|operator|infix|inline|external|suspend|expect|actual|reified|value"
                + "|annotation|enum|companion|context|in|out|class|interface|object|fun"
                + "|val|var|typealias|constructor|init|"
        var i = 0
        var steps = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (steps < 100000) {
            steps++
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 10 || cc === 13 || cc === 12) { i++; cc = c.peek(i); continue }
            if (cc === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) {
                i = i + 2
                cc = c.peek(i)
                while (cc >= 0 && cc !== 10 && steps < 100000) { steps++; i++; cc = c.peek(i) }
                continue
            }
            if (cc === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 42) {
                i = i + 2
                while (steps < 100000) {
                    steps++
                    var d = c.peek(i)
                    if (d < 0) { break }
                    if (d === 42 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) { i = i + 2; break }
                    i++
                }
                cc = c.peek(i)
                continue
            }
            break
        }
        if (cc === 64) { return undefined }
        var w = ""
        var k = 0
        while (k < 24) {
            var b2 = c.peek(i + k)
            var isWord = (b2 >= 48 && b2 <= 57) || (b2 >= 65 && b2 <= 90) || (b2 >= 97 && b2 <= 122) || b2 === 95
            if (!isWord) { break }
            w = w + String.fromCharCode(b2)
            k++
        }
        return (w !== "" && kws.indexOf("|" + w + "|") >= 0)
            ? undefined
            : abnf.newToken("\x01no-modifier-follows", 0)
    })()
~~) ;
// The trailing NotColon lets a soft-keyword modifier double as an identifier when it
// is actually a name: a real modifier is never immediately followed by `:`, but a
// parameter or property named `data`/`value`/`inner` is (data: Intent?). Without the
// guard [Modifiers] would eat the name and strand the `:`.
// `lateinit` is the third modifier that CHANGES what is emitted (see DataMod): the
// property it marks throws on a read before assignment instead of answering null, so
// PropDecl has to see it. This grammar has no pendingMods list, so it is a single
// walk-time flag the next property declaration takes.
Modifier    = ContextClause | CompanionMod
            | ( DataMod | "inner" ) KwEnd NotColon NotClassW
            | "lateinit" KwEnd NotColon <~~ noteLateinit() ~~>
            | ( "public" | "private" | "protected" | "internal" | "open" | "final"
              | "abstract" | "sealed" | "override" | "const"
              | "noinline" | "crossinline" | "tailrec"
              | "operator" | "infix" | "inline" | "external" | "suspend"
              | "expect" | "actual" | "reified" ) KwEnd NotColon ;
// `data` and `value` are the two modifiers that CHANGE what is emitted (they make the
// compiler synthesize componentN / equals / toString / copy), so ClassDecl has to see
// them. As ignorable modifiers they would be eaten by the Modifiers run in front of
// every declaration, hence the NotClassW guard: they only count as an ignorable
// modifier when no `class` follows - i.e. when they are really a name.
DataMod     = "data" | "value" ;
// `companion fun String.foo() {}` / `companion val Foo.bar get() = 1` - the
// CompanionBlocks proposal, where `companion` is an ordinary member MODIFIER. The
// `!"object"` lookahead keeps it away from a companion OBJECT declaration, which is a
// different production entirely; like every other modifier it is parsed and dropped, so
// the declaration lowers as the plain top level or extension declaration it looks like.
CompanionMod = "companion" KwEnd !"object" NotBrace NotColon ;
// A zero-width guard that fails when the next non-whitespace character is `{`. It keeps
// the `companion` MODIFIER off a `companion { ... }` BLOCK, which is a member of its own.
NotBrace    = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 10 || cc === 13) { i++; cc = c.peek(i) }
        return (cc === 123) ? abnf.newToken("\x01brace-follows", 0) : undefined
    })()
~~) ;
// context(A, B) / context(a: A, _: B) - Kotlin's context receivers and context
// parameters. They introduce ADDITIONAL implicit receivers, which the single-`this`
// model cannot express, so the clause is parsed and DROPPED: the declaration itself
// lowers as usual, and a body that really reads a context member does not resolve it.
// `context` is a soft keyword, so only a `(` immediately after it makes it one - a
// variable or a call named `context` is untouched (a Statement never runs Modifiers).
ContextClause = "context" KwEnd "(" [ CtxParam { "," CtxParam } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
// A context parameter may carry the parameter modifiers (context(noinline a: () -> Int)).
CtxParam    = { Annotation } [ Modifiers ] [ KId ":" ] Type ;
// An annotation: an optional use-site target, then either an @[...] set or a single
// entry (a possibly-qualified name with an optional balanced argument list).
Annotation  = "@" [ AnnTarget ":" ] ( AnnSet | AnnEntry ) ;
// Use-site targets are word-bounded, so @fieldOf stays a plain annotation named
// "fieldOf" rather than the target "field".
// ORDER MATTERS: the alternation is ordered choice and KwEnd sits AFTER it, so a
// shorter alternative that is a PREFIX of a longer one must come second - with "set"
// first, @setparam:x would match "set", KwEnd would fail on the `p`, and "setparam"
// would never be retried.
AnnTarget   = ( "field" | "property" | "setparam" | "set" | "get" | "receiver" | "param"
              | "delegate" | "file" | "all" ) KwEnd ;
AnnSet      = "[" { AnnEntry } "]" ;
// An annotation in STATEMENT position. Identical to Annotation except that its
// argument list is given up when a single `=` follows it, which is what tells
// `@Ann(var first) = x` (a destructuring declaration annotated with a bare @Ann)
// from `@Ann("") (var first) = x` (@Ann WITH arguments, then the pattern).
StmtAnn     = "@" [ AnnTarget ":" ] ( AnnSet | KId { "." KId } [ SkipParen NotAssign ] ) ;
// Each segment may carry type arguments - `@[foo<A, B>(a, b) foo.bar.doo<f>.foo<bar, goo>.foo]`
// in psi/annotation/Annotations.kt is one annotation set with a clean parse tree - so the
// angle run is skipped after every name, not only after the last.
AnnEntry    = KId [ SkipAngle ] { "." KId [ SkipAngle ] } [ AnnArgsOk SkipParen ] ;
// A zero-width guard that refuses an annotation argument list with an EMPTY argument,
// a dangling `=` or a lone `*` (psi/emptyArgumentsInAnnotations.kt). Twin of AnnArgsOk
// in kotlin-interpreter.abnf; see there.
AnnArgsOk   = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var steps = 0
        var ch = c.peek(i)
        while ((ch === 32 || ch === 9 || ch === 10 || ch === 13 || ch === 12) && steps < 100000) {
            steps++; i++; ch = c.peek(i)
        }
        if (c.peek(i) !== 40) { return undefined }
        i++
        var depth = 1
        var last = 40
        var bad = false
        while (steps < 200000) {
            steps++
            var b = c.peek(i)
            if (b < 0) { break }
            if (b === 32 || b === 9 || b === 10 || b === 13 || b === 12) { i++ }
            else if (b === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) {
                i = i + 2
                var d = c.peek(i)
                while (d >= 0 && d !== 10 && steps < 200000) { steps++; i++; d = c.peek(i) }
            }
            else if (b === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 42) {
                i = i + 2
                var e = c.peek(i)
                while (e >= 0 && steps < 200000) {
                    steps++
                    if (e === 42 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) { i = i + 2; break }
                    i++; e = c.peek(i)
                }
                last = 120
            }
            else if (b === 34 || b === 39) {
                var q = b
                i++
                var f = c.peek(i)
                while (f >= 0 && steps < 200000) {
                    steps++
                    if (f === 92) { i = i + 2; f = c.peek(i); continue }
                    if (f === q) { i++; break }
                    i++; f = c.peek(i)
                }
                last = 120
            }
            else if (b === 40) { depth++; i++; last = 120 }
            else if (b === 41) {
                depth--
                if (depth === 0) {
                    if (last === 61 || last === 42) { bad = true }
                    i++
                    break
                }
                i++; last = 120
            }
            else if (b === 44 && depth === 1) {
                if (last === 40 || last === 44 || last === 61 || last === 42) { bad = true; break }
                i++; last = 44
            }
            else { i++; if (depth === 1) { last = b } }
        }
        if (bad) { return abnf.newToken("\x01empty-annotation-argument", 0) }
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// package / import headers. The package name is parsed and ignored. Each import
// is resolved against the runtime builtins (core.stdlibImports); one that does
// not resolve aborts the compile, unless -warn-imports downgrades it to a
// warning (see resolveImport in the startScript). QualName also accepts a
// trailing .* wildcard. Neither pushes a statement, so buildMain never sees them.
Package     <~~ pop() ~~> = KwPackage QualName [ ";" ] ;
// resolveImport resolves, warns under -warn-imports, or aborts. The simple name each
// import brings in (and its `as` alias) is also recorded, which tells a supertype that
// is genuinely OUTSIDE the program apart from one the resolver merely failed to find:
// see noteImportName, and Import in kotlin-interpreter.abnf.
Import      <~~ ktImport(takeAll(), up.pos) ~~>
            = KwImport QualName [ KwAs KId <~~ push({ialias: up.in}) ~~> ] [ ";" ] ;
QualName    <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> = KId { "." KId } [ "." "*" ] ;

// A top level val/var: its declaration thunk becomes a top level statement. Unlike a
// LOCAL declaration it may carry a visibility-only accessor (private set), which the
// subset does not enforce but has to consume.
TopProp     = DelegateDecl | TopDecl | TopBareProp ;
// A top-level property with neither an initializer nor an accessor (an abstract or
// expect declaration, or a header split across files). It still binds its name, to
// undefined, so later references resolve; an extension receiver is dropped as
// everywhere else. The twin of TopBareProp in kotlin-interpreter.abnf; BareDecl is the
// LOCAL form.
TopBareProp <~~ push(makeBareDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd [ SkipAngle ] { Annotation } [ RecvSeg { RecvSeg } ]
              NotHardKw KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] [ ":" Type ] [ WhereClause ] [ "=" DiscardExpr ] [ ";" ] ;
TopDecl     <~~ ktAt = up.start; push(makeDecl(takeAll(), up.in)) ~~>
            = ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd NotHardKw KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" DeclType ] "=" Expression { PropVis } [ ";" ] ;

FunDecl     <~~ ktNoteBody(up.start, up.pos); ktNoteGeneric(c.srcSlice(up.start, up.pos), "fun"); push(makeFun(takeAll(), retLblTop(), reifiedTop())); retLblPop(); reifiedPop() ~~>
// The angle run AFTER the name - `fun naturalOrder<in T : Comparable<T>>(a: T, b: T)`
// (psi/examples/util/Comparison.kt, clean tree) - is where Kotlin's parser still accepts
// type parameters even though the language wants them before the name. Skipped, not
// erased into pendingReified: only the leading TypeParams position declares reified ones.
            = KwFun [ TypeParams ] KId <~~ push(up.in); retLblPush(up.in, "fun"); reifiedPush() ~~> [ SkipAngle ] ParamList [ ":" RetType ] { WhereClause | ContractClause } FunBody ;
// A function CONTRACT - fun f(): Int contract [ returns() implies (x != null) ] - is a
// static promise to the compiler's smart-cast analysis with no run-time effect, so it is
// parsed and dropped. Its entries are ordinary expressions (callsInPlace(...),
// `returns() implies (...)` through the user-infix rule), discarded by DiscardExpr.
ContractClause = KwContract "[" [ DiscardExpr { "," DiscardExpr } [ "," ] ] "]" ;
// The type-parameter list of a generic function. Type parameters are erased - except a
// `reified` one, which the body can TEST (`x is T`). Such a parameter is compiled to a
// hidden extra parameter carrying the type NAME as a string, filled in at every call
// site from its explicit type argument (see TypeArgs / makeCall), and `x is T` becomes
// the ordinary js_is_type probe against that string.
TypeParams  <~~ pendingReified = reifiedNamesOf(stripWs(up.in)) ~~>
            = SkipAngle ;
// A generic constraint clause - fun <T> larger(a: T, b: T): T where T : Comparable<T> = ...
// (and the class form, class Box<T> : Base() where T : Any). Type parameters are erased
// in the untyped handle world, so the whole clause is parsed and dropped; it pushes
// nothing. Kotlin allows several comma separated constraints (where T : A, T : B).
WhereClause = KwWhere TypeConstraint { "," TypeConstraint } ;
TypeConstraint = { Annotation } KId ":" Type ;
// A trailing `;` after a function body is ordinary Kotlin - psi/FunctionExpressions.kt
// spells `fun c() = fun name();` out with a clean tree.
FunBody     = Block [ ";" ] | "=" ExprBody [ ";" ] ;
ExprBody    <~~ push(makeExprBody(pop())) ~~>
            = Expression ;

// A top-level extension function WITH a body - fun Receiver.name(params): T { ... } -
// is genuine: it lowers to an IR function whose instance slot is the receiver (bound
// to `this`), declared under "ext$name"; the mcall suffix emitter dispatches
// recv.name(args) to it (see extResolves). A builtin or class method of the name
// always wins, and a call tests the receiver against the type ExtRecv records where
// that test is exact (the extension chain in emitSuffix). RecvSeg pushes nothing.
// The leading angle run is the function's own TYPE PARAMETERS, not a skippable
// decoration: `inline fun <reified T> Any.isA(): Boolean = this is T` resolves `is T`
// against the call site, exactly as the non-extension form does. TypeParams only fills
// pendingReified, so the item list makeFun reads is unchanged.
ExtFun      <~~ ktNoteBody(up.start, up.pos); push(makeExtFunStmt(takeAll(), retLblTop(), reifiedTop())); retLblPop(); reifiedPop() ~~>
            = KwFun [ TypeParams ] ExtRecv KId <~~ push(up.in); retLblPush(up.in, "fun"); reifiedPush() ~~> ParamList [ ":" Type ] [ WhereClause ] FunBody ;
ExtRecv     <~~ push({extRecv: up.in}) ~~>
            = AnnoRecv { RecvSeg } ;
// The receiver of an extension may carry annotations, including use-site targeted ones
// (fun @receiver:Ann @Other Int.f()). They are erased like every other annotation.
// The receiver may also be a PARENTHESIZED type - fun (() -> Unit).baz(),
// fun (C & Any).dnnF() - which is how a function type or an intersection is used as one.
RecvSeg     = NameRecvSeg | "(" Type ")" [ "?" ] "." ;
NameRecvSeg = KId [ SkipAngle ] [ "?" ] "." ;
// An annotation between `fun` and the receiver belongs to the RECEIVER TYPE, and
// Kotlin only allows it on a plain named one: `fun @receiver:A C?.f()` is accepted,
// while `fun @[a] foo()` (no receiver at all) and `fun <T> @ann (T & Any).f()` (a
// parenthesized receiver) are both errors its parser records as such. Requiring a
// NameRecvSeg behind the annotations is what keeps those two out.
AnnoRecv    = TypeAnno { TypeAnno } ( NameRecvSeg | FnRecvSeg | BareFnRecvSeg ) | RecvSeg ;
// An annotated receiver may also be an UNPARENTHESIZED function type:
// `fun @receiver:A () -> Unit?.functionWithAnnotatedReceiver2() {}` is a TYPE_REFERENCE
// whose child is a FUNCTION_TYPE, followed by the DOT - psi/annotationsOnNullableTypes.txt
// records exactly that, with no PsiErrorElement. It stays behind the annotation run (a
// bare `fun () -> Unit.f()` is not offered), so no shape the strict rules already refused
// becomes reachable.
BareFnRecvSeg = "(" [ FnTypeParam { "," FnTypeParam } [ "," ] ] ")" "->" Type "." ;
// The one PARENTHESIZED receiver an annotation may precede is a FUNCTION type -
// `fun @[a] (T.(A) -> R).foo()` parses, `fun <T> @ann (T & Any).foo8()` does not, and
// the corpus records exactly that split. The arrow is what tells them apart, so this
// rule spells out the function-type shapes rather than accepting any parenthesized
// type; an intersection or a plain grouped name falls through and is refused.
FnRecvSeg   = "(" FnRecvType ")" [ "?" ] "." ;
FnRecvType  = { TypeAnno } [ "suspend" KwEnd ] { TypeAnno }
              ( KId [ SkipAngle ] { "." KId [ SkipAngle ] } [ "?" ] RecvFnTail
              | "(" [ FnTypeParam { "," FnTypeParam } [ "," ] ] ")" "->" Type ) ;

// A function declaration outside the executable subset: a BODILESS extension or
// abstract declaration (abstract fun name(): R / fun Recv.name(): R). A NON-dotted
// fun WITH a body is deliberately NOT matched here: its body must parse in FunDecl,
// so a syntax gap inside the body surfaces at its true position instead of degrading
// the whole function into this notImpl. (Member-level extension functions also land
// here: they carry two receivers, which the single-`this` subset cannot model.)
UnsupFun    <~~ notImpl("extension or abstract function", up.pos) ~~>
            = KwFun [ SkipAngle ] ( ExtFunName SkipParen [ ":" Type ] [ WhereClause ] [ SkipBlock | "=" DiscardExpr ]
                                  | AnnoRecv { RecvSeg } [ SkipAngle ] SkipParen [ ":" Type ] [ WhereClause ] [ SkipBlock | "=" DiscardExpr ]
                                  | SkipParen [ ":" Type ] [ WhereClause ] [ SkipBlock | "=" DiscardExpr ]
                                  | KId [ SkipAngle ] SigParamsSkip [ ":" Type ] [ WhereClause ] ) [ ";" ] ;
// The parameter list of a BODILESS `fun name(...)` - the one UnsupFun alternative that
// is reached from a shape the strict rules refused for no structural reason. A plain
// SkipParen there would accept `fun foo(@Deprecated)`, whose whole point is that the
// parameter has no name (psi/annotation/noParameterYet.txt records
// "Expecting a parameter declaration"). This keeps the run balanced-and-skipped in
// every OTHER alternative and only demands a name here.
SigParamsSkip = "(" [ SigParamSkip { "," SigParamSkip } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
SigParamSkip = [ Modifiers ] [ KwVararg ] [ Modifiers ] [ ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd ] KId [ ":" Type ] [ "=" DiscardExpr ] ;
// The receiver of a member-level (or bodiless) extension function: annotated,
// parenthesized (fun (suspend () -> T).f()), nullable, dotted.
ExtFunName  = AnnoRecv { RecvSeg } KId [ SkipAngle ] { "." KId [ SkipAngle ] } ;

// A parameter list carries three things the call sites and the callee need:
// the NAMES (positional slots and named-argument resolution), the DEFAULT
// expressions, and the index of a vararg parameter. See makeParamList / emitParamSig.
ParamList   <~~ push(makeParamList(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" [ Param { "," Param } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
// `vararg` is NOT one of the ignorable Modifiers: it changes how the parameter binds
// (it collects the remaining call arguments), so it is matched on its own. Kotlin
// allows annotations on either side of it, hence the two Modifiers slots.
Param       <~~ ktAt = up.start; push(makeParam(takeAll())) ~~>
// The type is OPTIONAL and a `val`/`var` is accepted in front of the name. Both are
// semantic errors on an ordinary function, never syntactic ones: psi/ParameterType.kt
// (`fun test1(a) {}`, `fun test2(a = 4) {}`) and psi/operators/
// untilOperatorDifferentContexts.kt (`fun main(val x: Int = 0..<n) {}`) both carry a
// clean tree. Same spelling as the interpreter half's Param.
            = [ Modifiers ] [ KwVararg <~~ push({isvararg: true}) ~~> ] [ Modifiers ]
              [ ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd ] KId <~~ push({pname: up.in}) ~~> [ ":" PType ] [ "=" ParamDefault ] ;
ParamDefault <~~ push({pdef: pop()}) ~~>
            = CollLit | Expression ;
// A DECLARED type in a binding position - a parameter, a constructor parameter, a
// property - captured for its WIDTH. Types are erased here, but `val x: Long = 1`
// says the literal is a Long, and a property, a parameter, a parameter default, a
// field write and a return position all have to keep that (`w.x * 1000000 * 1000000`
// is 1000000000000). It is a WRAPPER production rather than a tag on Type itself
// because ~40 parents reference Type and a tag there would change how every one of
// them pops (see docs/abnf-dialect-gotchas.md). The twin is PType in
// kotlin-interpreter.abnf.
PType       <~~ push({pty: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Type ;
// The same capture in RETURN position: `fun ret(): Long = 1` answers a Long.
RetType     <~~ push({rty: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Type ;
// [1, 2] - Kotlin's collection literal. Legal only as an annotation argument or
// default; anywhere else it is a semantic error that the PARSER still accepts, which is
// what psi/CollectionLiterals.kt records. It is also an EXPRESSION here, and that does
// not collide with the bracketed destructuring pattern: a pattern is a DECLARATION and
// is always followed by `=`, and its entries are plain names, so DestructPat is simply
// tried first and rolls back. It already lowers to a list.
CollLit     <~~ push(makeCollLit(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "[" [ Expression { "," Expression } [ "," ] ] "]" ;

// class Counter(val start: Int, var step: Int) { var value = start; fun next() ... }
ClassDecl   <~~ ktNoteBody(up.start, up.pos); ktNoteClass(up.in, up.start, up.pos); ktNoteGeneric(c.srcSlice(up.start, up.pos), "class"); push(makeClassStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ DataMod <~~ push({dmod: up.in}) ~~> KwEnd ] KwClass KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] ( CtorModsOnly CtorParams | [ PrimaryCtorKw ] [ CtorParams ] ) [ ":" Supers ] [ WhereClause ] [ "{" { Member } "}" ] ;
// The primary constructor may be written out - class Foo constructor(x: Int) - with its
// own visibility and annotations (class A private @Inject constructor(...)). The keyword
// and its modifiers carry no meaning the subset models, so they are parsed and dropped;
// the parameter list that follows is the ordinary primary constructor.
PrimaryCtorKw = [ Modifiers ] KwConstructor ;
// `class foo private () : Bar` - the modifiers of a primary constructor written
// WITHOUT the `constructor` keyword. Kotlin's own parser accepts that shape (the psi
// corpus keeps it under declarations/ConstructorModifiers) but ONLY when a parameter
// list really follows: `open class AB private { ... }` is the error "Expecting
// list - dropping that pairing would accept files Kotlin rejects.
// Spelled as one-or-more so it can never match the empty string.
CtorModsOnly = ( Modifier | Annotation ) { Modifier | Annotation } ;

// The supertype list of a REAL (lowered) type declaration. Unlike the tag-less
// SuperList used by the skipped forms, this one pushes what the emitter needs:
// {supers: [{name, isCall, args, deleg}...]}. The FIRST supertype becomes the
// descriptor's __super, which is the chain the runtime walks for method lookup
// (memberCall), for `super.m()` (js_supercall) and for `is T` (js_is_type) - so
// single inheritance is genuine, and a class extending a class that implements an
// interface still answers `is Interface`. Any FURTHER supertype is an extra
// interface: its default methods are copied into the descriptor (so they are
// callable) but it is NOT on the __super chain, so `is SecondInterface` is false.
// `: I by expr` records the delegate; `: C(args)` marks a superCLASS construction.
Supers      <~~ push({supers: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = SuperItem { "," SuperItem } ;
SuperItem   <~~ push(makeSuper(takeAll())) ~~>
            = SuperName [ "(" [ SArg { "," SArg } [ "," ] ] ")" <~~ push({iscall: true}) ~~> | KwBy SDeleg ] ;
SuperName   <~~ push({sname: baseTypeName(stripWs(up.in))}) ~~> = Type ;
SArg        <~~ push({sarg: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;   // Positional only (a super constructor call).
// The delegate of `: I by expr` must NOT be a full Expression: a bare name followed by
// the class body - `: Greeter by base { override fun ... }` - would parse as the
// trailing-lambda call `base { ... }` and swallow the whole body. So the delegate is a
// postfix chain whose argument lists are always PARENTHESIZED, which covers every
// realistic form (by base, by this.impl, by makeIt(), by holder.get(0)).
SDeleg      <~~ push({sdeleg: pop()}) ~~> = DelegAdd ;
// The delegate may be a whole ARITHMETIC expression - psi/ByClauses.txt records
// `class A : b by a + b() * 5 { ... }` with a clean tree. These two levels are the
// ordinary AddExpr / MulExpr built on the NO-LAMBDA DelegChain, so precedence is the
// same while the class body still cannot be read as a trailing lambda. foldBinary
// returns a single operand unchanged, so `by base` is byte-identical to what it was.
// The twin of NoLamAdd / NoLamMul in kotlin-interpreter.abnf.
DelegAdd    <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = DelegMul { ( "+" | "-" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> DelegMul } ;
DelegMul    <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = DelegChain { ( "*" | "/" | "%" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> DelegChain } ;
DelegChain  <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "(" Expression ")" | RawStr | Template | KHexLit | KBinLit | FloatLit | KIntLit | CharLit | True | False | Null | ObjectExpr | DelegCall | VarRef ) { DelegMSfx | FieldSfx | IndexSfx } ;
DelegCall   <~~ push(makeCall(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] "(" [ CallArgs ] ")" ;
DelegMSfx   <~~ push({kind:"mcall", name: popName(), args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "." KId <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] "(" [ CallArgs ] ")" ;
CtorParams  <~~ push({cparams: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" [ CtorParam { "," CtorParam } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
// A default value (= expr) is genuine, exactly like a function Param's (common in data
// classes - data class Spec(val action: String = "", val flags: Int = 0)).
CtorParam   <~~ ktAt = up.start; push(makeCtorParam(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ Modifiers ] [ KwVararg <~~ push({isvararg: true}) ~~> ] [ ( "val" | "var" ) <~~ push({vv: up.in}) ~~> KwEnd ] KId <~~ push({pname: up.in}) ~~> ":" PType [ "=" ParamDefault ] ;

// A nested typealias is erased exactly like a top-level one.
Member      = [ Modifiers ] ( InitBlock | CompanionBlk | CompanionObject | SecondaryCtor | NestedClass | NestedType | TypeAlias | MemberExtProp | MemberExtDelegProp | AccessorProp | UnsupAccessor | PropDelegate | PropDecl | FunDecl <~~ push({method: pop()}) ~~> | MemberExtFun | MemberExtAbs | AbstractFun | UnsupFun ) ;
// A MEMBER extension function - fun B.ext() declared INSIDE a class - is the only
// construct with TWO receivers: the extension receiver, bound to `this` in the body,
// and the DISPATCH receiver, the enclosing class instance. The lowering keeps both:
// the function is installed on the descriptor under "ext$name" and takes
// (dispatchReceiver, extensionReceiver, args...), with the dispatch receiver bound in
// the frame as `__dispatch` so an unqualified member of the declaring class and
// `this@Class` still resolve. Being a member, it is in scope only where an instance of
// the declaring class is the enclosing receiver, which is what js_ktmextcall tests.
// The twin of MemberExtFun in kotlin-interpreter.abnf.
MemberExtFun <~~ push({memberExt: makeFun(takeAll(), retLblTop())}); retLblPop() ~~>
            = KwFun [ SkipAngle ] AnnoRecv { RecvSeg } KId <~~ push(up.in); retLblPush(up.in, "fun") ~~> [ SkipAngle ] ParamList [ ":" Type ] [ WhereClause ] [ ContractClause ] [ WhereClause ] FunBody ;
// The BODILESS member extension - `fun Int.qty(): String` in an interface and
// `abstract fun Int.scaled(): Int` in an abstract class. Like AbstractFun it
// contributes no implementation; the override in the subclass installs the real
// ext$name entry, which the lookup reaches from the runtime receiver. Ordered AFTER
// MemberExtFun so a member extension WITH a body keeps its own error positions.
MemberExtAbs <~~ takeAll() ~~>
            = KwFun [ SkipAngle ] AnnoRecv { RecvSeg } KId [ SkipAngle ] SigParamsSkip [ ":" Type ] [ WhereClause ] [ ContractClause ] [ WhereClause ] [ ";" ] ;
// A member function WITHOUT a body: an abstract method of an abstract class, or an
// interface method with no default. Nothing is emitted for it (the overriding class
// provides the implementation), but its NAME and arity are recorded, which is what
// interface delegation (`: I by impl`) needs to synthesize its forwarders. Tried
// after FunDecl, so a function that HAS a body never lands here. The trailing `;` is
// the ordinary statement separator (`interface I { fun f(): Int; fun g(): Int = 1 }`).
AbstractFun <~~ push({absmethod: makeAbstract(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = KwFun [ SkipAngle ] KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ParamList [ ":" Type ] [ WhereClause ] [ ";" ] ;
// `companion { ... }` - the CompanionBlocks proposal's anonymous companion. Its
// members become statics of the enclosing class, which the single-descriptor model does
// not express, so the block is parsed and reported as not implemented.
CompanionBlk <~~ notImpl("companion block", up.pos) ~~>
            = KwCompanion SkipBlock ;
// A nested CLASS - class Nested { } / inner class Inner { } inside another class or
// object - is genuine. Its descriptor is built exactly like a top-level one, and the
// enclosing descriptor gets a factory under the nested name, so `Outer.Nested()` and
// `outerInstance.Inner()` construct it. For an `inner` class the factory stores the
// enclosing instance as __outer, which is what `this@Outer` reads (see makeThisAt).
NestedClass <~~ push({nested: makeNested(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = [ "inner" KwEnd <~~ push({isinner: true}) ~~> ] ClassDecl ;
// A nested TYPE that is not a plain class - an enum class, an interface, an object
// declaration or an annotation class inside another class. GENUINE, and the twin of
// NestedType in kotlin-interpreter.abnf: the declaration is lowered exactly like a
// top-level one (so it binds under its own simple name), and the enclosing descriptor
// then gets the resulting DESCRIPTOR under the nested name, so `Outer.Kind.LEFT` and
// `Outer.Nested.member` resolve. A nested class keeps its own rule above, because a
// class needs a FACTORY (it is constructed) rather than the descriptor itself.
// Each alternative names the REAL builder, never the notImplemented fallback, so
// makeNestedValue can rely on lastDeclaredType having just been set.
NestedType  = NestedEnum | NestedIface | NestedObj | NestedErased | NestedTypeSkip ;
NestedEnum  <~~ push({nested: makeNestedValue(pop())}) ~~> = EnumReal ;
NestedIface <~~ push({nested: makeNestedValue(pop())}) ~~> = InterfaceReal ;
NestedObj   <~~ push({nested: makeNestedValue(pop())}) ~~> = ObjectReal | ObjectNoBody ;
// An annotation class is ERASED (see AnnotationClass): it declares metadata only, so
// there is no descriptor to attach. The erased thunk is dropped here.
NestedErased <~~ pop() ~~> = AnnotationClass ;
// The fallback for a nested type declaration whose SHAPE is outside the subset (an
// enum with an entry body, an interface with a primary constructor, a data class the
// class rule could not take). Recognised, reported as not implemented and skipped;
// notImpl pushes nothing, so makeClassStmt simply skips it.
NestedTypeSkip <~~ notImpl("nested type declaration", up.pos) ~~>
            = [ KwEnum | KwAnnotation | DataMod KwEnd ] KwClass KId [ SkipAngle ] [ CtorParamsSkip ] [ ":" SuperList ] [ SkipBlock ]
            | [ KwFun ] KwInterface KId [ SkipAngle ] [ ":" SuperList ] [ SkipBlock ]
            | KwObject KId [ ":" SuperList ] [ SkipBlock ] ;
// A trailing visibility-only accessor (var x = 0; private set) is parsed and ignored:
// the subset does not enforce visibility, so the property still binds its initializer.
// A body-carrying accessor (get() = ..., set(v) { ... }) has a `(` after get/set and is
// left to PropAccessor (not implemented); PropVis's NotParen guard declines it.
// The initializer is optional: a bodiless property (abstract var/val, lateinit var, or an
// interface property) has no `= init`; makeProp then defaults it to null so the field
// still exists - an implicit `name = ...` in a method resolves to this.name, and a read
// before assignment yields null (the flat, dispatch-by-name model has no real subclass).
// The modifier run is TAKEN at the name (nothing between `val` and the name notes a
// modifier, and every alternative Member tries after this one either drains the run
// itself or ignores it), so `lateinit` reaches makeProp instead of leaking into the
// next declaration that reads pendingMods.
PropDecl    <~~ ktAt = up.start; push(makeProp(takeAll(), up.in)) ~~>
            = ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd KId <~~ push(up.in); push({pmods: takeLateinit()}) ~~> [ ":" PType ] [ "=" Expression ] { PropVis } [ ";" ] ;
PropVis     = [ Modifiers ] ( KwGet | KwSet ) NotParen ;
// `by lazy { init }` is special-cased (eager, see LazyProp); every other delegate goes
// through Kotlin's real DELEGATE PROTOCOL - the delegate object is stored in a hidden
// `name$delegate` field on the instance and the descriptor gets a js_defprop accessor
// pair whose getter calls getValue(thisRef, property) and whose setter calls
// setValue(thisRef, property, value). An expression that is not a delegate at all (no
// getValue on its class chain) is reported as not implemented when the DECLARATION
// runs - js_ktdcheck, the twin of delegCheck in kotlin-interpreter.abnf, so the message
// and the -warn-unsupported placeholder are identical in both halves. An EXTENSION
// delegated property (val Recv.x by ...) has no single owner to pass as thisRef and
// stays unsupported.
PropDelegate = LazyProp | RealPropDelegate | UnsupDelegate ;
LazyProp    <~~ ktAt = up.start; push(makeProp(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "val" KwEnd KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" Type ] KwBy KwLazy [ SkipParen ] LazyInit { PropVis } [ ";" ] ;
// A delegated MEMBER property may still declare accessors - `val p: Int by A()` on one
// line and `get() = 1` on the next (psi/propertyDelegate/PropertyWithGetter.kt), or
// `val a by A(); get() = 1;` (psi/propertyDelegate/GetterInSameLine.kt). Kotlin parses
// both with a clean tree and rejects them SEMANTICALLY, so they are consumed and DROPPED
// here. The run has to be HERE: once RealPropDelegate has matched up to the delegate it
// succeeds, and ordered choice never offers the shape to UnsupDelegate.
RealPropDelegate <~~ push({accprop: makeDelegProp(takeAll(), up.pos)}) ~~>
            = ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd KId <~~ push({apname: up.in}) ~~> [ ":" Type ] KwBy DelegSrc [ ";" ] { DropAcc } { PropVis } [ ";" ] ;
DelegSrc    <~~ push({dsrc: pop()}) ~~>
            = Expression ;
// A delegated property may still declare accessors - `val p: Int by A()` on one line and
// `get() = 1` on the next (psi/propertyDelegate/PropertyWithGetter.kt), or
// `val a by A(); get() = 1;` (psi/propertyDelegate/GetterInSameLine.kt). The accessors are
// DROPPED (DropAcc): this alternative reports the whole property as not implemented, and
// an item left on the value stack would be collected by the enclosing class body.
UnsupDelegate <~~ notImpl("delegated property", up.pos) ~~>
            = ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd [ { TypeAnno } RecvSeg { RecvSeg } ] KId [ ":" Type ] KwBy DiscardExpr [ SkipBlock ] [ ";" ] { DropAcc } { PropVis } [ ";" ] ;
DropAcc     <~~ pop() ~~> = Accessor ;
// The lazy initializer lambda, wrapped so it lowers to "build the closure, call it
// once (no args)" - the property's init thunk. EAGER: the value is computed at the
// declaration, not on first access (single-threaded, deterministic). Deviation: a
// lazy initializer that forward-references a not-yet-initialized property sees it
// unset (real Kotlin defers past that); write lazy inits over already-bound names.
LazyInit    <~~ push(makeLazyInit(pop())) ~~>
            = Lambda ;

// Type is parsed and (apart from steering as/is) ignored. Generics (<...>) and the
// nullable ? are accepted and dropped; dotted names and function types too. A leading
// type annotation (@Composable () -> Unit) is accepted. A parenthesized group without
// a trailing -> is a grouped type, so a nullable function type ((Intent) -> Unit)?
// parses: the inner function type is the group, then ? applies to the whole. A leading
// `suspend` (a suspending function type: suspend () -> Unit, suspend Recv.() -> R) is
// accepted and dropped - KwEnd stops it from eating a `suspend`-prefixed type name.
// The nullable marker carries a NotColon guard so the `?` of an ELVIS operator is never
// mistaken for it: in `num as? Int ?: 0` the cast's type is `Int`, not `Int?` - without
// the guard Type ate the `?` and stranded the `:`. It is accepted both BEFORE a receiver
// tail (C?.() -> C: the nullable receiver of a lambda type) and after the whole type.
// A trailing `& Type` run is Kotlin's definitely-non-null type (T & Any); like every
// other type detail it is erased.
// The `?` before the tail is offered only to a RECEIVER tail ((A)?.() -> B), never to a
// plain `->`: `((@A C))? -> C` is a genuine syntax error in Kotlin - psi/
// TypeModifiersParenthesized.txt records a PsiErrorElement ("Property getter or setter
// expected") exactly there - and a `[ "?" NotColon ]` in front of the whole group
// silently accepted it. The interpreter half spells the same split as NullRecvFnTail.
Type        = { TypeAnno | "suspend" KwEnd | ContextClause } ( "(" [ FnTypeParam { "," FnTypeParam } [ "," ] ] ")" ( "->" Type | [ "?" NotColon ] RecvFnTail )
            | "(" FnTypeParam { "," FnTypeParam } [ "," ] ")" [ "?" NotColon ]
            | NotHardKw KId [ SkipAngle ] { "." KId [ SkipAngle ] } [ "?" NotColon ] [ "?" NotColon ] [ RecvFnTail ] ) [ "?" NotColon ] { "&" Type } ;
// A function-type parameter: an optional name (Kotlin allows named function-type
// parameters, onRun: (root: Boolean) -> Unit) then the type. Also serves the
// grouped-type case (a bare type with no name, in parentheses without a following ->).
FnTypeParam = [ KId ":" ] Type ;
// A receiver function type tail: Recv.() -> R / Recv.(A) -> R (a lambda-with-receiver
// type, e.g. @Composable RowScope.() -> Unit). The receiver type is the dotted name;
// this consumes the `.(params) -> Ret`. Parsed and ignored like any type.
RecvFnTail  = "." "(" [ FnTypeParam { "," FnTypeParam } [ "," ] ] ")" "->" Type ;
// A type annotation. Three details, each one a corpus cluster:
//   * a use-site target is allowed here too (@receiver:A R.() -> T).
//   * the annotation-array form @[A B C] is a type annotation like any other.
//   * the argument list must be ADJACENT to the name. Kotlin distinguishes
//     `@Foo(x)` (annotation WITH an argument list) from `@Foo (x)` (a bare
//     annotation on the parenthesized type `(x)`) by exactly that space, which
//     is why the psi corpus keeps the two under annotation/withParentheses and
//     annotation/withoutParentheses. Whitespace is therefore switched off
//     around the optional argument list - and switched back on unconditionally
//     afterwards, so a rolled-back alternative never leaks the setting.
//     The NotArrow guard stays on top of that: `@Composable() -> Unit` leaves
//     the `()` to the function type.
TypeAnno    = "@" [ AnnTarget ":" ] ( AnnSet | KId { "." KId } :whitespace() [ SkipParen NotArrow ] :whitespace(Whitespace) ) ;

// Every statement production pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> undefined | signal.
// A statement may carry a label (loop@ ...); the label is parsed and ignored, so the
// labelled statement still lowers. Labelled jumps (break@l / continue@l / return@l)
// route to notImpl (the target label cannot be resolved in the subset).
Statement   <~~ push(stmtPos(pop())) ~~>
            = { StmtAnn } ( ReturnAt | Return | BreakAt | Break | ContinueAt | Continue
            | Labeled | PlainStmt ) ;
PlainStmt   = Block | LocalFun | LocalModDecl | LocalType | Destructure | DelegateDecl | Decl | BareDecl | If | While | Do | For | TryStmt | Throw | Empty | ExprStmt ;
// A local declaration carrying MODIFIERS (`private val x = 1`, `inline fun f() {}`
// inside a body). Kotlin keeps the run and the declaration as ONE statement, which is
// what NewStmtLine below needs to be true before it can refuse the genuine same-line
// files. It sits AFTER the unmodified forms so the common path is untouched, and ModRun
// requires at least one real modifier so an ordinary statement never reaches it twice.
// The modifiers are parsed and DROPPED (Modifiers is item-neutral here).
LocalModDecl = ModRun LocalModTarget ;
ModRun      = ( Modifier | AnnEnumMod | VarianceMod ) Modifiers ;
LocalModTarget = LocalFun | Destructure | DelegateDecl | Decl | BareDecl ;
Labeled     <~~ push(makeLabeled(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = LabelDef PlainStmt ;
// A LOCAL type declaration - `class Local`, `interface I`, `object O`, `enum class E`,
// `typealias x = t` inside a function body. These reuse the top level productions
// verbatim (they already push a statement thunk), so a local class is lowered exactly
// like a top level one and the widened forms still route to notImplemented. Without
// it the words `class` / `interface` / `object` would be read as plain variable
// REFERENCES and the declaration fall apart into two junk expression statements.
LocalType   = [ Modifiers ] ( UnsupType | ClassDecl | UnsupFun ) ;
// A local function declaration (fun name(...) { ... } inside a block): genuine. It
// reuses makeFunStmt, which declares an own IR function's closure (over the current
// scope, so it captures enclosing locals and can recurse) under the name in the
// CURRENT scope. Comes before ExprStmt, which would read `fun` as an identifier.
// A LOCAL extension function (fun Int.tripled(): Int = this * 3, inside a block) is
// genuine too: ExtFun already pushes a whole statement thunk that declares "ext$name"
// in the CURRENT scope, so the mcall suffix emitter finds it exactly like a top-level
// one (dispatch is by name). Tried first because a non-dotted fun fails RecvSeg and
// backtracks into the plain form.
LocalFun    = ExtFun | LocalPlainFun ;
LocalPlainFun <~~ push(makeFunStmt(pop())) ~~>
            = FunDecl ;

// try/catch/finally and throw are implemented: throw calls js_throw (a Go panic the
// runtime unwinds), and each try/catch/finally body is compiled to its own closure
// handed to js_try (see makeTry). The catch clauses are DISCRIMINATED by their
// declared type: all of them go into one closure whose body is a js_ktexcmatch chain,
// and a value no clause matches is rethrown. A return/break/continue that leaves a try or catch
// body becomes a control signal that excDispatch re-issues in the enclosing frame
// (compile-core). Each part is marked so makeTry can tell them apart.
TryStmt     <~~ push(makeTry(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwTry TryBody { CatchClause } [ FinallyClause ] ;
TryBody     <~~ push({trybody: pop()}) ~~>
            = Block ;
CatchClause <~~ push(makeCatch(takeAll())) ~~>
// The catch parameter may carry a trailing comma - `catch (e: Exception,)`,
// psi/trailingCommaAllowed.kt, clean tree - as in the interpreter half.
            = KwCatch "(" KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" CatchTy ] [ "," ] ")" Block ;
// The DECLARED type of the catch parameter, kept so emitCatchClosure can pick the
// matching clause. Tagging it means CatchClause pushes three items instead of two,
// which is why this grammar overrides the shared makeCatch by assignment.
CatchTy     <~~ push({ctype: up.in}) ~~>
            = Type ;
FinallyClause <~~ push({finbody: pop()}) ~~>
            = KwFinally Block ;
Throw       <~~ push(makeThrow(pop())) ~~>
            = KwThrow Expression [ ";" ] ;

Block       <~~ push(makeBlockStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" [ Statement ] { NewStmtLine Statement } "}" ;
// Kotlin refuses two statements on ONE line without a `;` between them. Twin of
// NewStmtLine in kotlin-interpreter.abnf; see there.
NewStmtLine = :script(~~
    (function() {
        if (c.peek(-1) === 59) { return undefined }
        var i = 0
        var steps = 0
        while (steps < 100000) {
            steps++
            var ch = c.peek(i)
            if (ch < 0) { return undefined }
            if (ch === 10 || ch === 59 || ch === 125) { return undefined }
            if (ch === 32 || ch === 9 || ch === 13 || ch === 12) { i++; continue }
            if (ch === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) { return undefined }
            if (ch === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 42) {
                var d = 1
                i = i + 2
                while (d > 0 && steps < 100000) {
                    steps++
                    var b = c.peek(i)
                    if (b < 0) { return undefined }
                    if (b === 10) { return undefined }
                    if (b === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 42) { d = d + 1; i = i + 2; continue }
                    if (b === 42 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) { d = d - 1; i = i + 2; continue }
                    i++
                }
                continue
            }
            return abnf.newToken("\x01same-line-statement", 0)
        }
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;


Decl        <~~ ktAt = up.start; push(makeDecl(takeAll(), up.in)) ~~>
            = ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd NotHardKw KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" DeclType ] "=" Expression [ ";" ] ;
// `val x: Int` / `var r` with no initializer. Kotlin's PARSER accepts this - the
// missing initializer is a later, semantic error ("must either have a type annotation
// or be initialized") - and the psi corpus expects the file parsed, so the name is
// declared as undefined. The `!"="` lookahead keeps this from swallowing a declaration
// whose INITIALIZER failed to parse: that must stay a parse error, not become a
// silently value-less binding.
BareDecl    <~~ ktAt = up.start; push(makeBareDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd NotHardKw KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" DeclType ] !"=" [ ";" ] ;
// The declared type of a local/top-level val or var. Types are erased, with ONE
// exception that is observable: a lambda-with-receiver type (Int.(Int) -> Int) says the
// lambda's first argument is its `this`, which changes how the lambda is emitted and how
// `recv.name(args)` on the variable dispatches. So the type text is captured and
// inspected for the `.(` receiver marker; everything else about it is dropped.
DeclType    <~~ var dt = stripWs(up.in); pendingRecvLambda = isRecvFnType(dt); push({dtype: dt}) ~~>
            = Type ;
// A local or top-level delegated property (val x by <delegate>). Same split as
// PropDelegate: `val x by lazy { init }` is special-cased (LazyDecl - the initializer
// is lowered once and x becomes an ordinary binding); every other delegate goes
// through the delegate protocol (RealDelegateDecl), and UnsupDelegateDecl keeps a
// placeholder so the rest still lowers under -warn-unsupported.
DelegateDecl = LazyDecl | RealDelegateDecl | UnsupDelegateDecl ;
LazyDecl    <~~ ktAt = up.start; push(makeDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "val" KwEnd KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" Type ] KwBy KwLazy [ SkipParen ] LazyInit [ ";" ] ;
// The same delegate protocol for a local or top-level `val x by d`: the binding holds
// a delegate BOX (js_ktdbox), and every read (js_ktget) and every write (js_ktvarset)
// goes through getValue/setValue. thisRef is null, as it is in Kotlin for a property
// with no owner. The twin of RealDelegateDecl in kotlin-interpreter.abnf.
RealDelegateDecl <~~ push(makeDelegDecl(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" Type ] KwBy DelegSrc [ ";" ] [ AccRun ] [ ";" ] ;
UnsupDelegateDecl <~~ push(notImplStmt("delegated property", up.pos)) ~~>
            = ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd [ { TypeAnno } RecvSeg { RecvSeg } ] KId [ ":" Type ] KwBy DiscardExpr [ SkipBlock ] [ ";" ] { DropAcc } [ ";" ] ;

If          <~~ push(makeIf(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwIf "(" Expression ")" Statement [ KwElse Statement ] ;

While       <~~ push(makeWhile(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = KwWhile "(" Expression ")" Statement ;

Do          <~~ push(makeDo(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = KwDo Statement KwWhile "(" Expression ")" [ ";" ] ;

// for (i in 1..n) and for (x in list); the range is tried first. Ranges come as
// 1..n (inclusive), 0 until n (exclusive) and n downTo 1, all with an optional
// positive step (soft keywords: until/downTo/step stay usable as names). The
// destructuring form for ((a, b) in pairs) is tried last (it opens with a second
// paren, which the other two reject).
For         = ForRange | ForList | ForDestruct ;
ForRange    <~~ push(makeForRange(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwFor "(" KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> KwIn AddExpr RangeOp AddExpr [ StepSfx ] ")" Statement ;
// `..<` (Kotlin 1.9's open-ended range) must be tried before `..`, which would match
// its first two characters and strand the `<`. It means exactly what `until` means.
RangeOp     = "..<" <~~ push({rop: "until"}) ~~>
            | ".." <~~ push({rop: ".."}) ~~>
            | KwUntil <~~ push({rop: "until"}) ~~>
            | KwDownTo <~~ push({rop: "downTo"}) ~~> ;
StepSfx     <~~ push({step: pop()}) ~~>
            = KwStep AddExpr ;
ForList     <~~ push(makeForList(pop(), pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = KwFor "(" KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> KwIn Expression ")" Statement ;
// A destructuring for-loop, for ((px, py) in points): genuine. Each element is
// destructured with the componentN convention (the same one val (a, b) = e uses), so
// data classes and hand-written component1/component2 both work.
ForDestruct <~~ push(makeForDestruct(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwFor "(" DTuple KwIn Expression ")" Statement ;
DTuple      <~~ push({dnames: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = DPat ;

// The destructuring PATTERN, shared by the declaration, the for-loop and the lambda
// parameter list. Kotlin's name-based destructuring proposal widens the classic
// `(a, b)` into two bracketings that mean DIFFERENT things:
//   (a, b)              parenthesized - POSITION based, the classic componentN form
//   (val a, var b)      parenthesized with per-entry val/var - NAME based: each entry
//                       reads the property of its own name, so order does not matter
//   (val b = second)    ... optionally RENAMING: `b` is the new name, `second` the
//                       property that is read (the short form drops the val/var)
//   [a, b] / [val a]    bracketed - always POSITION based, whatever the entries say
// Both bracketings take a trailing comma and per-entry annotations and types.
// Each entry pushes {dn, dprop}: dprop is "" for a positional entry and the property
// name for a name-based one, which is all the lowering below needs.
DPat        = "(" DEntryN { "," DEntryN } [ "," ] ")"
            | "[" DEntryP { "," DEntryP } [ "," ] "]" ;
// The leading pattern of a declaration written WITHOUT an outer val/var
// (`(val a, val b) = x`, `[val a] = x`). The parenthesized form must open with a
// val/var entry - otherwise `(a) = x`, which is not a Kotlin declaration at all,
// would be read as one.
DPatLead    = "(" DEntryD { "," DEntryN } [ "," ] ")"
            | "[" DEntryP { "," DEntryP } [ "," ] "]" ;
DEntryN     <~~ push(makeDEntry(takeAll(), true)) ~~>
            = { Annotation } [ DValVar ] DEntName [ ":" Type ] [ "=" DEntProp ] ;
DEntryD     <~~ push(makeDEntry(takeAll(), true)) ~~>
            = { Annotation } DValVar DEntName [ ":" Type ] [ "=" DEntProp ] ;
DEntryP     <~~ push(makeDEntry(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = { Annotation } [ DValVar ] DEntName [ ":" Type ] [ "=" DEntProp ] ;
DValVar     = ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd <~~ push({dk: "kw", ds: ""}) ~~> ;
DEntName    = KId <~~ push({dk: "name", ds: up.in}) ~~> ;
DEntProp    = KId <~~ push({dk: "prop", ds: up.in}) ~~> ;

// `return` inside a lambda argument returns from the ENCLOSING FUNCTION, not from the
// lambda (Kotlin's non-local return, which its inline higher-order functions rely on:
// xs.forEach { if (...) return it }). Whether this return is the non-local kind is a
// purely lexical question, decided here at walk time from the return-target stack: the
// top entry is a lambda's label exactly when we are inside a lambda argument.
Return      <~~ var nl = inLambdaBody(); push(makeReturn(takeAll(), nl)) ~~>
            = KwReturn NoSpacedAt [ Expression ] [ ";" ] ;

Break       <~~ push(makeBreak()) ~~>    = KwBreak NoSpacedAt [ ";" ] ;
Continue    <~~ push(makeContinue()) ~~> = KwContinue NoSpacedAt [ ";" ] ;

Empty       <~~ push(function(b) { return b }) ~~>
            = ";" ;

ExprStmt    <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~>
            = Expression [ ";" ] ;

// Every expression production pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> value.
// An ANNOTATED assignment - `when (1) { 1 -> @ann46 x46 += foo46() }` (psi/annotation/at/
// blockLevelExpressions.kt, clean tree). A statement carries its annotations through
// Statement's own { StmtAnn } run; a when arm / if branch holds an EXPRESSION, and the
// AnnExpr level below only reaches a UnaryExpr, so the `+=` of `@ann x += ...` would be
// stranded. The annotations push nothing, so this is item-neutral.
Expression  = AnnAssign | SuperAssign | Assignment | CallAssign | OrExpr ;
AnnAssign   = Annotation { Annotation } ( SuperAssign | Assignment | CallAssign ) ;
// `super.p = v` - the superclass's SETTER. Twin of SuperAssign in
// kotlin-interpreter.abnf; see there.
SuperAssign <~~ push(makeSuperAssign(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwSuper [ SuperQual ] "." KId <~~ push({sfname: up.in}) ~~>
              ( "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "%=" | "=" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> Expression ;
Assignment  <~~ ktAt = up.start; push(makeAssign(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target ( "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "%=" | "=" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> Expression ;
// An assignment whose left side is a postfix expression containing a call, so a plain
// Target (KId rooted, dot/index steps) cannot cover it: foo(x).field = y,
// items(k)[0] += 1. GENUINE - the chain up to the last step is evaluated once and the
// last step is written like any other slot, exactly as makeAssign writes p.obj/p.key.
// A chain that ENDS in a call is not an lvalue, and only that case stays unimplemented
// (both sides still run, so their calls stay visible in a call graph). Tried only after
// Assignment fails - i.e. when a `(` call breaks the chain.
CallAssign  <~~ push(makeCallAssign(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = CallChain ( "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "%=" | "=" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> Expression ;
// The same postfix chain as CallMember, but kept UNFOLDED so the assignment can split
// off the last step: everything before it is the container, and the last `.name` or
// `[i]` is the slot written. The twin of CallChain in kotlin-interpreter.abnf.
CallChain   <~~ push({chain: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = Primary { MethodSfx | SafeMethodSfx | FieldSfx | SafeFieldSfx | IndexSfx | "!!" } ;

Target      <~~ push(makeTargetRef(takeAll())) ~~>
// `?.` and `!!` are accepted in a TARGET as well - `t?.i++` and `t!!.i++` are ordinary
// postfix targets in Kotlin's own tree (psiUtil/isSelectorInExpression.txt). Both are the
// identity in the untyped handle world, so the key is recorded exactly as for `.`.
            = KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "." KId <~~ push({key: up.in}) ~~> | "?." KId <~~ push({key: up.in}) ~~> | "!!" | TIndex } ;
TIndex      <~~ push({idx: kIndexArg(takeAll(), up.pos)}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression { "," Expression } [ "," ] "]" ;

// if as an expression needs both arms.
IfExpr      <~~ push(makeIfExpr(pop(), pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = KwIf "(" Expression ")" ( ValueBlock | Expression ) KwElse ( ValueBlock | Expression ) ;

// when { c1 -> e1; else -> e } and when (x) { 1, 2 -> e1; else -> e }.
WhenExpr    <~~ push(makeWhen(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwWhen [ "(" WhenSubj ")" ] "{"
              { WhenElse | WhenArm } "}" ;
// The subject is a plain expression, or a val/var binding - when (val x = e) { ... } -
// whose name is in scope in every arm body. The type annotation is parsed and ignored.
WhenSubj    = WhenBind | WhenBindDestr | WhenExprSubj ;
// The initializer may be missing (when (val x: T) { }); the subject is Unit then.
// Kotlin's own tree also records an extension RECEIVER on the subject variable
// (when (val T.x5 = foo) { }) - a PROPERTY with a TYPE_REFERENCE before the DOT,
// semantically meaningless but syntactically accepted. psi/WhenWithSubjectVariable.kt.
WhenBind    <~~ var e = pop(); push({subj: e, subjName: pop()}) ~~>
            = { Annotation } ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd { WhenBindRecv } KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" Type ] "=" Expression
            | { Annotation } ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd { WhenBindRecv } KId <~~ push(up.in); push(makeConst(undefined)) ~~> [ ":" Type ] ;
WhenBindRecv = KId [ SkipAngle ] "." ;
// A DESTRUCTURING subject variable - when (val (x, y) = foo) { ... }. The components are
// parsed and dropped (nothing binds them), so only the subject EXPRESSION is genuine;
// the arms still see the value they compare against. psi/WhenWithSubjectVariable.kt.
WhenBindDestr <~~ push({subj: pop()}) ~~>
            = { Annotation } ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd SkipParen "=" Expression ;
WhenExprSubj <~~ push({subj: pop()}) ~~>
            = Expression ;
// The else arm must be tried first: otherwise 'else' would parse as a variable arm
// (and only fail at run time, when no other arm matches).
WhenElse    = KwElse "->" ( ValueBlock | Expression ) <~~ push({els: pop()}) ~~> [ ";" ] ;
WhenArm     <~~ push(makeWhenArm(takeAll())) ~~>
            = WhenCond { "," WhenCond } [ "," ] "->" ( ValueBlock | Expression ) [ ";" ] ;
// A branch condition: an inclusive range test (in / !in), a type test (is T / !is T,
// undecidable so it never matches and warns), or a value to compare with.
WhenCond    = WhenIn | WhenNotIn | WhenIs | WhenVal ;
WhenIs      <~~ var t = pop(); push({isType: true, neg: pop(), tname: t, pos: up.pos}) ~~> = IsKw TypeName ;
WhenIn      <~~ var h = pop(); push({lo: pop(), hi: h, neg: false}) ~~>
            = KwIn AddExpr ".." AddExpr ;
WhenNotIn   <~~ var h = pop(); push({lo: pop(), hi: h, neg: true}) ~~>
            = "!" KwIn AddExpr ".." AddExpr ;
WhenVal     <~~ push({v: pop()}) ~~>
            = Expression ;

OrExpr      <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), true)) ~~>
            = AndExpr { "||" AndExpr } ;

AndExpr     <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = EqExpr { "&&" EqExpr } ;

// Referential equality === / !== is lowered like structural == / != (in the untyped
// handle world both are identity for objects), so the operands still run and fold.
EqExpr      <~~ push(foldBinary(ktEqOps(takeAll(), up.start, up.pos))) ~~>
            = RelExpr { EqOp RelExpr } ;
EqOp        = "===" <~~ push("==") ~~> | "!==" <~~ push("!=") ~~>
            | "==" <~~ push("==") ~~> | "!=" <~~ push("!=") ~~> ;

RelExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = InExpr { ( "<=" | ">=" | "<" | ">" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> InExpr } ;

// x in coll / x !in coll (membership): binds tighter than comparison, as in Kotlin.
// Lowered to coll.contains(x) via js_mcall (the same call the interpreter makes), so
// both engines agree; !in negates. The receiver is a list here.
InExpr      <~~ push(makeInExpr(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = TypeOp { InOp TypeOp } ;
// The `in` operator must sit on its LEFT OPERAND's line, so a `when` arm's value never
// swallows the next arm's `in 10..99 ->`. Twin of InOp in kotlin-interpreter.abnf; see
// there.
InOp        = SameLine InOpKw ;
InOpKw      = "!" KwIn <~~ push({inop: "!in", at: up.pos}) ~~> | KwIn <~~ push({inop: "in", at: up.pos}) ~~> ;

// The type-TEST operator level: x is T / x !is T, lowered to the dynamic js_is_type
// probe (makeIsCheck). It sits directly under `in`, exactly where Kotlin's
// infixOperation puts `is`, and above elvis - so `a ?: b is T` is `(a ?: b) is T`.
// The CAST operator `as` binds far tighter (see AsExpr); keeping the two apart is what
// lets `num as? Int ?: 0` parse (the cast is the elvis' LEFT operand, not its result).
// The suffix is optional, so plain expressions are unaffected.
TypeOp      <~~ push(applyTypeOp(takeAll())) ~~> = Elvis [ IsSfx ] ;
// A postfix `is T` needs two guards, both defending the `when`-arm boundary where a
// bare `is T` leads the NEXT arm. SameLine: the `is` must sit on the operand's line, so
// an arm value never reaches across the newline to swallow the next arm's leading
// `is T,` (a multi-condition arm) - otherwise `... -> v` then `is B,` on the next line
// parses as `v is B` and strands the comma. NotArrow: on the SAME line, `is T ->` still
// leads an arm (is CellInfoGsm -> ...), so the postfix must not fire there either.
// Together they leave same-line `x is T` (not arrow-led) as the only postfix type test.
IsSfx       <~~ var t = pop(); push({isType: true, neg: pop(), tname: t, pos: up.pos}) ~~> = SameLine IsKw TypeName NotArrow ;
AsSfx       <~~ var t = pop(); push({asType: true, safe: pop(), tname: t}) ~~> = AsKw TypeName ;
IsKw        = KwIs <~~ push(false) ~~> | KwNotIs <~~ push(true) ~~> ;
AsKw        = KwAsSafe <~~ push(true) ~~> | KwAs <~~ push(false) ~~> ;
TypeName    <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> = Type ;
// A zero-width guard that fails when the next non-whitespace token is `->`.
NotArrow    = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 10 || cc === 13) { i++; cc = c.peek(i) }
        return (cc === 45 && c.peek(i + 1) === 62)
            ? abnf.newToken("\x01arrow-follows", 0)
            : undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// A zero-width guard that fails at end of line (a newline or a // comment before
// the next token): the value of a labelled return must start on the SAME line,
// otherwise `return@lbl` followed by another statement would swallow that
// statement as its return value (newlines are insignificant everywhere else).
SameLine    = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc === 32 || cc === 9) { i++; cc = c.peek(i) }
        return (cc === 10 || cc === 13 || (cc === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47))
            ? abnf.newToken("\x01newline-follows", 0)
            : undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// A zero-width guard that fails when the next non-whitespace token is `(` - used to
// tell a visibility-only accessor (private set) from a body accessor (set(v) { ... }).
NotParen    = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 10 || cc === 13) { i++; cc = c.peek(i) }
        return (cc === 40) ? abnf.newToken("\x01paren-follows", 0) : undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// A zero-width guard that fails when the next word is one of Kotlin's own - so a
// user infix operator can never be a keyword. The list holds the hard keywords plus the
// soft ones that open a declaration or a clause (a name would be indistinguishable from
// an infix operator there); anything else is a legal simpleIdentifier.
NotKeyword  = :script(~~
    (function() {
        // The DECLARATION MODIFIERS that are soft keywords are deliberately NOT here:
        // Kotlin reads `x7 infix x9` and `@Ann() class C annotation y` as infix calls
        // (psi/recovery/binaryExpression/SoftModifierAsInfixFunction.txt). The
        // VISIBILITY modifiers and get/set/init/constructor/companion stay in: `var x =
        // 0` followed on the SAME line by `private set` is an accessor run, and an
        // infix reading would swallow it. Same list as the interpreter half's NotInfixKw.
        var reserved = "|as|break|class|continue|do|else|false|for|fun|if|in|interface|is|null"
            + "|object|package|return|super|this|throw|true|try|typealias|val|var|when|while"
            + "|by|where|get|set|init|constructor|companion|catch|finally|import"
            + "|private|public|protected|internal|step|until|downTo"
            + "|to|and|or|xor|shl|shr|ushr|"
        // Skip spaces, tabs AND comments: the operand of an infix call may be written
        // `12 /*true*/c 12` (psiUtil/isCallee.kt), and a guard that stops at the `/`
        // reads an EMPTY word and refuses a perfectly good infix operator - the
        // "keyword-rejecting guard must skip COMMENTS" trap in docs/abnf-dialect-
        // gotchas.md, met from the refusing side.
        var i = 0
        var steps = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (steps < 20000) {
            steps++
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9) { i++; cc = c.peek(i); continue }
            if (cc === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 42) {
                i = i + 2
                cc = c.peek(i)
                while (steps < 20000 && cc >= 0 && !(cc === 42 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47)) {
                    steps++
                    i++
                    cc = c.peek(i)
                }
                if (cc < 0) { break }
                i = i + 2
                cc = c.peek(i)
                continue
            }
            break
        }
        var w = ""
        while ((cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || cc === 95) {
            w = w + String.fromCharCode(cc)
            i++
            cc = c.peek(i)
        }
        return (w === "" || reserved.indexOf("|" + w + "|") >= 0)
            ? abnf.newToken("\x01keyword-follows", 0)
            : undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// A zero-width guard that fails when the next word is `class`, so `data`/`value` are
// left to ClassDecl (which needs them) instead of being consumed as ignorable modifiers.
NotClassW   = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 10 || cc === 13) { i++; cc = c.peek(i) }
        return (cc === 99 && c.peek(i + 1) === 108 && c.peek(i + 2) === 97
                && c.peek(i + 3) === 115 && c.peek(i + 4) === 115)
            ? abnf.newToken("\x01class-follows", 0)
            : undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// A zero-width guard that fails when the next non-whitespace token is `:` - used so a
// soft-keyword modifier (data/value/inner/...) that is really a parameter or property
// NAME is not consumed as a modifier (a genuine modifier is never followed by `:`).
NotColon    = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 10 || cc === 13) { i++; cc = c.peek(i) }
        return (cc === 58) ? abnf.newToken("\x01colon-follows", 0) : undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// A zero-width guard that fails when the next non-whitespace token is a single `=`
// (`==` and `=>` do not count). It separates an annotation's ARGUMENT list from a
// parenthesized destructuring pattern that merely follows the annotation: in
// `@Ann(var first) = x` the parens are the pattern, in `@Ann("") (var first) = x`
// they are the arguments. Trying the argument list first and rejecting it when an
// `=` follows resolves the two exactly the way the Kotlin parser does.
NotAssign   = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 10 || cc === 13) { i++; cc = c.peek(i) }
        if (cc !== 61) { return undefined }
        var nx = c.peek(i + 1)
        return (nx === 61 || nx === 62) ? undefined : abnf.newToken("\x01assign-follows", 0)
    })()
~~) ;

// a ?: b yields a unless it is null (the elvis operator; the right side only runs then).
Elvis       <~~ ktAt = up.start; push(makeElvis(takeAll(), up.in)) ~~>
            = InfixExpr { "?:" InfixExpr } ;

// A stdlib infix call - a to b (Pair), a and b / a shl b - binds tighter than elvis
// and looser than +/-, matching Kotlin. All seven are genuine: `to` builds a Pair
// (read via .first/.second), and/or/xor lower to js_jband/js_jbor/js_jbxor (the
// non-short-circuit boolean operators on two booleans, int32 bit operations
// otherwise), shl/shr/ushr to the int32 shift externs. Only these built-in infix
// names are recognised; the names stay usable as identifiers (KwEnd word boundary).
InfixExpr   <~~ push(makeInfix(takeAll())) ~~>
            = RangeExpr { InfixOp RangeExpr } ;
InfixOp     = ( "to" | "and" | "or" | "xor" | "shl" | "shr" | "ushr" ) <~~ push({iop: up.in}) ~~> KwEnd
            | UserInfix ;
// A USER-declared `infix fun` used as an operator: `a dot b` is `a.dot(b)`, dispatched
// through js_mcall like any method call. This is Kotlin's own rule (its
// infixFunctionCall is `rangeExpression (simpleIdentifier rangeExpression)*`), and the
// two guards are what make it safe here:
//   SameLine   - the operator must sit on the left operand's line, so a statement on the
//                NEXT line is never read as an infix call (newlines are otherwise
//                insignificant in this grammar)
//   NotKeyword - the name must not be one of Kotlin's own words, so `x else y`,
//                `x is T`, `x as T`, `val`, `get`, ... keep their meaning
// The step/until/downTo range words bind one level DOWN (RangeExpr), which is tried
// first and consumes them, so they never reach here either.
UserInfix   = SameLine NotKeyword KId <~~ push({iop: up.in, user: true}) ~~> ;

// A range expression as a VALUE - a..b, a..<b, a until b, a downTo b, a step b - in
// general operand position (`3 in 1..5`, `for (i in r)`, `(0..n).map { ... }`). It
// lowers to js_ktrange / js_ktrestep, which build the same first-class progression
// value the interpreter half has (see the PROGRESSIONS block of abnf/jsrtkotlin.go).
// Without a range operator RangeExpr is a pure pass-through (its single AddExpr thunk
// is returned unchanged), so ordinary expressions stay byte-identical. The for-loop and
// the when(x){ in a..b } forms bind their bounds at AddExpr, below this level, so they
// keep splitting on their own range token and never build a range value.
RangeExpr   <~~ push(makeRange(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = AddExpr { RangeInfix AddExpr } ;
RangeInfix  = ( "..<" | ".." ) <~~ push({rop: up.in}) ~~>
            | KwUntil <~~ push({rop: "until"}) ~~>
            | KwDownTo <~~ push({rop: "downTo"}) ~~>
            | KwStep <~~ push({rop: "step"}) ~~> ;

AddExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(ktAdoptPlus(takeAll(), up.in, up.start))) ~~>
            = MulExpr { ( "+" | "-" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> MulExpr } ;

MulExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AsExpr { ( "*" | "/" | "%" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> AsExpr } ;

// The cast level: x as T / x as? T. Kotlin's asExpression binds tighter than every
// binary operator and looser than the prefix unary, so `a + b as Int` is
// `a + (b as Int)` and `num as? Int ?: 0` is `(num as? Int) ?: 0`. `as` is an
// unchecked identity in the untyped handle world; `as?` yields null on a mismatch
// (js_is_type decides). Casts chain left to right (x as A as B).
AsExpr      <~~ push(applyTypeOp(takeAll())) ~~> = UnaryExpr { AsSfx } ;

// A leading annotation on an EXPRESSION - when (@Ann "") { ... }, @Ann x += f() -
// is legal wherever an expression starts. Annotations are erased, so an annotated
// expression IS the expression; AnnExpr pushes nothing of its own.
UnaryExpr   = AnnExpr | PreIncDecRef | PreIncDec | Neg | Not | PostfixExpr ;
// `++a::b` is `++(a::b)` - `::` binds tighter than the prefix operator - so the operand
// is a callable reference, not the assignable Target that PreIncDec wants.
// psi/DoubleColon.kt spells it next to `-a::b` and `a+b::c`; the reference has no
// runtime value in this subset, so the whole form is reported. It is tried FIRST and
// declines on the missing `::`, which leaves ordinary `++a` to PreIncDec unchanged.
PreIncDecRef <~~ takeAll(); push(notImplExpr("callable reference on an increment target", up.pos)) ~~>
            = ( "++" | "--" ) Target "::" RefName ;
// PREFIX ++x / --x. Kotlin has both fixities and they differ in their VALUE: the postfix
// form yields the old value, the prefix form the new one. It must precede Neg, or `--a`
// reads as a double negation - which quietly gives the right answer for a number and
// never assigns. The twin of PreIncDec in kotlin-interpreter.abnf.
PreIncDec   <~~ push(makePreIncDec(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "++" | "--" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> Target ;
AnnExpr     = Annotation { Annotation } UnaryExpr ;
Neg         <~~ push(kNegFold(pop(), up.in)) ~~>
            = "-" UnaryExpr ;
// The operand of a prefix `!` must be on the `!`'s own line. Kotlin ends the statement
// at the newline, so `true!!!` followed by `true!!!!` on the next line is a POSTFIX_
// EXPRESSION plus a stray `!` that its parser records as
// "Unexpected tokens (use ';' to separate expressions on the same line)" -
// psi/AssertNotNull.txt. Without the guard the stray `!` would negate the NEXT statement
// and the file parse clean.
Not         <~~ push(makeNot(pop())) ~~>
            = "!" SameLine UnaryExpr ;

PostfixExpr = IncDec | CallMember ;
IncDec      <~~ push(makeIncDec(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target ( "++" | "--" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ;

// Counter(5) is a constructor call, this.m() and list.add() are method calls,
// p.x is a property read, list[i] an indexed read. The ?. variants yield null
// (instead of failing) when their receiver is null. A method call may take a
// trailing lambda - list.map { it * 2 } - which becomes the last argument.
// A trailing "!!" (non-null assertion) is consumed and dropped: in the untyped handle
// world it is the identity, so x!! lowers exactly like x (and x!!.m() still chains).
CallMember  <~~ ktAt = up.start; push(foldCallMember(takeAll(), up.in)) ~~>
            = Primary { MethodSfx | SafeMethodSfx | FieldSfx | SafeFieldSfx | IndexSfx | GenericSfx | DotAnonSfx | InvokeSfx | RefSfx | "!!" } ;
// A callable reference or class literal whose RECEIVER is an arbitrary expression:
// `X()::class`, `object {}::class`, `foo()::bar`. CallableRef in Primary covers only a
// dotted NAME on the left (`a.b::c`), so these three lines of psi/DoubleColon.kt need
// this. The value is not modelled - a class literal has no runtime representation in
// this subset - so it is reported.
// RefName PUSHES its text, so the tag drains it before pushing the suffix record.
RefSfx      <~~ push({kind: "refsfx", name: pop()}) ~~>
            = "::" RefName ;
RefName     = KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> | "class" KwEnd <~~ push("class") ~~> ;
// A call's EXPLICIT TYPE ARGUMENTS, captured for `xs.filterIsInstance<String>()`.
// Twin of MTypeArgs in kotlin-interpreter.abnf; see there.
MTypeArgs   <~~ push({mtargs: up.in}) ~~>
            = SkipAngle ;
MethodSfx   <~~ var mc = popMCall(); push({kind:"mcall", name: mc.name, targ: mc.targ, args: takeAll()}); retLblPop() ~~>
            = "." KId <~~ push({mname: up.in}); retLblPush(up.in, "lam") ~~> [ MTypeArgs ] MArgs ;
SafeMethodSfx <~~ var mcs = popMCall(); push({kind:"mcall", name: mcs.name, targ: mcs.targ, args: takeAll(), safe: true}); retLblPop() ~~>
            = "?." KId <~~ push({mname: up.in}); retLblPush(up.in, "lam") ~~> [ MTypeArgs ] MArgs ;
FieldSfx    = "." KId <~~ push({kind:"field", name: up.in}) ~~> ;
SafeFieldSfx = "?." KId <~~ push({kind:"field", name: up.in, safe: true}) ~~> ;
// An EXPLICIT TYPE ARGUMENT LIST in expression position that is NOT followed by a call:
// `p(qux<a, b>)`, `a.foo<T> -> a` in a when arm, `Foo<out Int>` as a statement. Kotlin
// resolves the `a < b` ambiguity with a continuation/stop-token rule (the same rule Dart
// states verbatim in its own corpus - see docs/abnf-dialect-gotchas.md); InstAhead below
// implements it, and the run itself is parsed and dropped, so the suffix is item-neutral
// and foldCallMember pops exactly what it did before.
GenericSfx  = InstAhead SkipAngle ;
// A zero-width guard deciding whether the `<` at the parse position opens a type
// argument list rather than a comparison. It scans a BALANCED angle run built only of
// type-shaped characters and accepts only when the token AFTER the closing `>` cannot
// follow a comparison's right operand: a stop token ( ) ] } , ; : . ? end of line, end
// of file ) or a continuation token ( ( . == != -> ). `a < b` never reaches a closing
// `>`; `a < b && c > d` bails on the `&`; `f(a < b, c > d)` closes but is followed by
// `d`, so all three keep parsing as comparisons.
InstAhead   = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(0)
        var steps = 0
        // The guard sees RAW input, so the whitespace and comments in front of the `<`
        // are still ahead of the position and have to be stepped over here.
        while (steps < 4000) {
            steps = steps + 1
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10 || cc === 12) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i); continue }
            if (cc === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) {
                while (cc >= 0 && cc !== 10) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i) }
                continue
            }
            if (cc === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 42) {
                i = i + 2
                cc = c.peek(i)
                while (cc >= 0 && !(cc === 42 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47)) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i) }
                if (cc < 0) { return abnf.newToken("\x01unterminated-comment", 0) }
                i = i + 2
                cc = c.peek(i)
                continue
            }
            break
        }
        if (cc !== 60) { return abnf.newToken("\x01no-angle", 0) }
        // An EMPTY run is "Type expected" in Kotlin, not an instantiation: psi/Super.kt
        // spells `super<>.foo()` out as a PsiErrorElement, so the guard must decline it
        // and leave the `<` to the comparison operator that will fail on it.
        if (c.peek(i + 1) === 62) { return abnf.newToken("\x01empty-angle", 0) }
        var depth = 0
        var n = 0
        while (n < 4000) {
            n = n + 1
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc < 0) { return abnf.newToken("\x01eof-in-angle", 0) }
            if (cc === 60) { depth = depth + 1; i = i + 1; continue }
            if (cc === 62) {
                depth = depth - 1
                i = i + 1
                if (depth === 0) { break }
                continue
            }
            // `->` of a function type argument, HashMap<String, () -> Unit>.
            if (cc === 45 && c.peek(i + 1) === 62) { i = i + 2; continue }
            var ok = (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 48 && cc <= 57)
                  || cc === 95 || cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10
                  || cc === 44 || cc === 46 || cc === 63 || cc === 42 || cc === 40 || cc === 41
                  || cc === 58 || cc === 64 || cc === 91 || cc === 93
            if (!ok) { return abnf.newToken("\x01not-a-type", 0) }
            i = i + 1
        }
        if (depth !== 0) { return abnf.newToken("\x01unbalanced-angle", 0) }
        cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc === 32 || cc === 9) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i) }
        if (cc < 0) { return undefined }
        if (cc === 41 || cc === 93 || cc === 125 || cc === 44 || cc === 59 || cc === 58
            || cc === 46 || cc === 40 || cc === 63 || cc === 10 || cc === 13) { return undefined }
        if (cc === 61 && c.peek(i + 1) === 61) { return undefined }
        if (cc === 33 && c.peek(i + 1) === 61) { return undefined }
        if (cc === 45 && c.peek(i + 1) === 62) { return undefined }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01not-instantiation", 0)
    })()
~~) ;
// The index operator takes an argument LIST (`a[i, j]` is `get(i, j)`), and the list
// takes a trailing comma like every other one (psi/trailingCommaAllowed.kt writes
// `x[ 1, 3 , ]`). Only the ONE-argument form is implemented; a wider one is accepted
// and reported, rather than silently indexing by its first argument.
IndexSfx    <~~ push({kind:"index", idx: kIndexArg(takeAll(), up.pos)}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression { "," Expression } [ "," ] "]" ;
// The argument list must OPEN on the callee's own line. Kotlin's own tree settles this:
// psi/DoubleColon.txt has `a::b.c` followed by a blank line and then `(a::b)()`, and
// records them as TWO statements, not as one call of `c`. A trailing LAMBDA is
// deliberately not guarded: Kotlin does allow it after a line break.
// SEVERAL trailing lambdas - `f() {} {} {}` / `f {} {} {}` (psi/CallWithManyClosures.kt,
// clean tree). Kotlin allows only one SEMANTICALLY; its parser makes each one an
// argument, and so does the interpreter half.
MArgs       = SameLine "(" [ CallArgs ] ")" { TrailLambda } | TrailLambda { TrailLambda } ;
// CALLING a parenthesized expression - `(f)()`, `(f) {} {} {}`, `(a::b)()` - and calling
// the RESULT of a call, `anon()()`. `f()` and `f { }` are matched by Call, which is keyed
// on a NAME; once the callee is an arbitrary expression there is no name, so the call
// becomes a SUFFIX of the member chain and the value in hand is invoked. The twin of
// ParenApply in kotlin-interpreter.abnf.
ParenApply  <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ParenSeed InvokeLike { InvokeLike } ;
ParenSeed   = "(" Expression ")" ;
InvokeLike  = InvokeSfx | TrailLamSfx | GenericSfx ;
// SameLine, for the reason MArgs carries it: psi/DoubleColon.txt records a `(` that
// OPENS a line as a new statement, not as the previous expression's argument list.
InvokeSfx   <~~ push({kind:"invoke", args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = SameLine "(" [ CallArgs ] ")" { TrailLambda } ;
TrailLamSfx <~~ push({kind:"invoke", args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = TrailLambda { TrailLambda } ;
// `receiver.{ ... }` / `receiver.(expr)` - the anonymous-method syntax of very early
// Kotlin (psi/examples/With.txt, psi/CallsInWhen.txt, psi/DoubleColon.txt, all clean
// trees). The construct no longer exists in the language, so the body is consumed and
// the step is reported rather than guessed at.
DotAnonSfx  <~~ push({kind:"dotanon", t: notImplExpr("anonymous method", up.pos)}) ~~>
            = "." ( SkipBlock | "(" DiscardExpr ")" ) [ SkipParen ] ;

// A trailing lambda is the call's LAST argument, so it is wrapped in the same
// {at} record the other arguments use.
// It may carry ANNOTATIONS, which is also Kotlin's OLD label syntax: `x2.filter @f
// { return 2 }` is a LAMBDA_ARGUMENT whose child is an ANNOTATED_EXPRESSION wrapping the
// lambda (psi/newLabels/oldSyntaxExpressions.txt). Annotation pushes nothing, so the
// record still wraps exactly one lambda.
TrailLambda <~~ push({at: pop()}) ~~>
            = { Annotation } Lambda ;
// A call's argument list. Kotlin has three forms and they can be mixed:
//   f(x)            positional
//   f(name = x)     NAMED - resolved against the callee's parameter names at emit
//                   time, when the whole file has been walked (see orderArgs)
//   f(*arr)         SPREAD - the elements of arr become individual arguments
// A named argument must be tried before the plain expression: `d = 7` would
// otherwise parse as the assignment expression it looks like.
CallArgs    = CallArg { "," CallArg } [ "," ] ;
CallArg     = NamedArg | SpreadArg | PlainArg ;
NamedArg    <~~ push({at: pop(), aname: pop().argn}) ~~>
            = KId <~~ push({argn: up.in}) ~~> "=" Expression ;
SpreadArg   <~~ push({at: pop(), spread: true}) ~~>
            = "*" Expression ;
PlainArg    <~~ push({at: pop()}) ~~>
            = Expression ;

// RawStr before Template (both open with "), FloatLit before IntLit (a plain integer
// falls through), AnonFun/CallableRef before Call/VarRef (they would otherwise eat the
// leading identifier). AnonFun and qualified callable references are recognised, not
// lowered.
// IfExpr/WhenExpr/TryExpr live here (before Call/VarRef, which would read the `if`/
// `when`/`try` keyword as an identifier) as their ONLY expression home, so each is a
// primary: it can be an operand - a + if (c) x else y, "n=" + when (k) { ... } - AND
// take a postfix chain - when (x) { ... }.also { ... }, try { ... }.getOrNull(). The
// else/arm bodies are full Expressions, so `if (c) a else b + 1` still binds b + 1 as
// the else. A leading `if` at statement level is the If statement (matched before
// ExprStmt); a leading `when` is this expression form.
Primary     = DeclExpr | CollLit | ParenApply | "(" Expression ")" | RawStr | Template | KHexLit | KBinLit | DotFloatLit | FloatLit | KIntLit | CharLit | True | False | Null | Lambda | AnonFun | AnonFunUnsup | CallableRef | ObjectExpr | IfExpr | WhenExpr | TryExpr | ThrowExpr | ReturnAtExpr | BreakAtExpr | ContinueAtExpr | ReturnExpr | BreakExpr | ContinueExpr | SuperCall | SuperField | SuperRef | ThisExpr | LabeledPrim | Call | VarRef ;

// super.m(args) - the base implementation of an overridden method. It compiles to
// js_supercall(<the DEFINING class's superclass descriptor>, this, "m", args), which
// walks the __super chain from there; taking the defining class (known while its
// methods are emitted) rather than this.__class is what stops an override from
// calling itself when the receiver is a further subclass. Outside a class body there
// is no superclass, so it is reported as not implemented.
SuperCall   <~~ push(makeSuperCall(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
// SkipAngle1, not SkipAngle: `super<>` is a genuine syntax error ("Type expected" in
// psi/Super.txt), and an EMPTY angle pair would otherwise be accepted here.
            = KwSuper [ SuperQual ] "." KId <~~ push({smname: up.in}) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] "(" [ CallArgs ] ")" ;
// `super<I>` NAMES which supertype the call means, and a class with two supertypes
// needs it - `class C : I1, I2 { override fun f() = super<I1>.f() }`. The name is
// recorded here and makeSuperCall / makeSuperField start the __super walk at that
// descriptor.
SuperQual   <~~ push({squal: superQualName(up.in)}) ~~>
            = SkipAngle1 ;
// `super.x` as a VALUE - `override val v: Int get() = super.v + 1`. Kotlin's rule is
// that it runs the SUPERCLASS's getter, so the walk starts at the defining class's
// superclass descriptor exactly as super.m() does; a supertype that declares the
// property as a plain field has no accessor on the chain, and the read then lands on
// the instance's own backing slot (js_ktsupget). Ordered AFTER SuperCall (which needs
// a `(`) and BEFORE SuperRef, which is the bare `super`.
SuperField  <~~ push(makeSuperField(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwSuper [ SuperQual ] "." KId <~~ push({sfname: up.in}) ~~> ;
// A BARE `super`, optionally qualified - `super<sdf>` as a parameter default in
// psi/SimpleExpressions.kt, clean tree. There is no super OBJECT in this subset (only
// the super CALL above), so the reference is recognised and reported. SkipAngle1 rather
// than SkipAngle: it demands at least one item inside the angles, so a comparison
// against an empty pair of brackets cannot be swallowed. Twin of the interpreter's
// SuperExpr.
SuperRef    <~~ push(notImplExpr("super reference", up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwSuper [ SkipAngle1 ] ;
SkipAngle1  = "<" ( SkipAngle | "->" | SkipAngleItem ) { SkipAngle | "->" | SkipAngleItem } ">" ;

// `throw e` in VALUE position - Kotlin types it Nothing, so it is a legal expression:
// fun bail(m: String): Nothing = throw AppError(m), or `val x = a ?: throw E()`. It
// emits the same js_throw the statement form does and then leaves a dead block, so the
// value it "returns" is never observed. Statement-level `throw` still matches the Throw
// statement first (PlainStmt is tried before ExprStmt), so nothing changes there.
ThrowExpr   <~~ push(makeThrowExpr(pop())) ~~>
            = KwThrow Expression ;

// `return`, `break` and `continue` in EXPRESSION position - `val v = map[k] ?: return`,
// `val line = it.next() ?: break`. Each is Nothing-typed: it never yields a value, it
// transfers control out of the whole surrounding expression. In IR that is exactly what
// the STATEMENT forms already emit - a ret, or a branch to the enclosing loop's exit /
// head - so each expression form is the statement thunk wrapped to hand back a {b, v}
// pair whose block is DEAD and whose value is therefore unreachable (the same shape
// makeThrowExpr uses). A phi merging the placeholder is valid and never taken.
// The twin of ReturnExpr / BreakExpr / ContinueExpr in kotlin-interpreter.abnf; NotAtLbl
// declines the labelled spellings, which ReturnAt / BreakAt / ContinueAt own.
// The LABELLED spellings in expression position - psi/SimpleExpressions.kt puts
// `break@la` and `continue@la` in a parameter default with a clean tree. Each is the
// statement emitter wrapped to hand back {b, v}, exactly as above, so the label
// resolution and the control-signal handling stay in one place.
ReturnAtExpr <~~ push(exprOfStmt(makeReturnAt(takeAll(), up.pos))) ~~>
            = KwReturn AtAdjacent "@" KId <~~ push({rlbl: up.in}) ~~> [ SameLine Expression ] ;
BreakAtExpr <~~ push(exprOfStmt(makeBreakTo(pop().lbl, up.pos))) ~~>
            = KwBreak AtAdjacent "@" KId <~~ push({lbl: up.in}) ~~> ;
ContinueAtExpr <~~ push(exprOfStmt(makeContinueTo(pop().lbl, up.pos))) ~~>
            = KwContinue AtAdjacent "@" KId <~~ push({lbl: up.in}) ~~> ;
ReturnExpr  <~~ var nl = inLambdaBody(); push(makeReturnExpr(takeAll(), nl)) ~~>
            = KwReturn NotAtLbl NoSpacedAt [ SameLine Expression ] ;
BreakExpr    <~~ push(makeBreakExpr()) ~~>    = KwBreak NotAtLbl NoSpacedAt ;
ContinueExpr <~~ push(makeContinueExpr()) ~~> = KwContinue NotAtLbl NoSpacedAt ;
// A LABEL on an ordinary operand - `1 + label3@ 3 + 4`, `l1@ foo bar l2@ baz`. Kotlin's
// own tree (psi/newLabels/basic.txt) records each as a LABELED_EXPRESSION wrapping the
// operand, so the label binds at the PRIMARY level. It names nothing on an ordinary
// operand, so it is consumed and dropped and the operand is its own value.
LabeledPrim <~~ var t = pop(); pop(); push(t) ~~>
            = LabelDef Primary ;
NotAtLbl    = :script(~~
    (function() {
        return (c.peek(0) === 64) ? abnf.newToken("\x01labelled", 0) : undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// try { ... } catch (e) { ... } used as an EXPRESSION (val x = try { ... } catch ...,
// or an expression-body function fun f() = try { ... } catch ...) is genuine. Its bodies
// are VALUE blocks - their last expression is their value - wrapped so the body closure
// RETURNS that value; js_try passes the winning clause's return value straight back, so
// the value of the whole try-expression is simply js_try's result. A return/break/
// continue leaving a body still travels as a control signal, which excDispatch re-issues
// exactly as in the statement form. The finally clause is a statement block: it runs for
// its effect and cannot change the value (unless it returns, which js_try honours).
// Must precede Call/VarRef so `try` is not read as an identifier.
TryExpr     <~~ push(makeTryValue(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwTry TryVBody { CatchVClause } [ FinallyClause ] ;
TryVBody    <~~ push({trybody: valueBody(pop())}) ~~>
            = ValueBlock ;
CatchVClause <~~ push(makeCatch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCatch "(" KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" Type ] [ "," ] ")" CatchVBody ;
CatchVBody  <~~ push(valueBody(pop())) ~~>
            = ValueBlock ;

// An anonymous object expression - object [: Supertypes] { members } - in value
// position, e.g. passed as an argument (object : IHasComponent<Navigator> { ... }).
// GENUINE: each evaluation builds a fresh instance whose descriptor holds the
// methods and whose fields are the initialized properties. The method closures
// close over the expression site's scope (js_closure env, like a lambda; the
// interpreter twin captures the same chain), and property initializers run in a
// child scope holding the new object as `this`. Supertypes are parsed and ignored
// (no inheritance; passing the object where an interface is expected still works
// because js_mcall dispatches by name). A body outside the member subset falls
// back to ObjectExprSkip (notImplemented). Must precede Call/VarRef, which would
// otherwise read `object` as an identifier.
// A type DECLARATION sitting where an expression belongs - `val p = 1 < interface I {}`
// (psi/recovery/InterfaceInExpressionPosition). It is an error, but Kotlin's parser
// still builds the declaration there rather than giving up, and the corpus expects the
// file parsed. It is skipped and reported as notImplemented. Deliberately NOT including
// `object`, which ObjectExpr already owns as a genuine expression.
DeclExpr    <~~ push(notImplExpr("declaration in expression position", up.pos)) ~~>
            = ( [ KwFun ] KwInterface | KwClass | KwEnum KwClass | KwAnnotation KwClass ) KId
              [ SkipAngle ] [ CtorParamsSkip ] [ ":" SuperList ] [ SkipBlock ] ;
ObjectExpr  = ObjectExprReal | ObjectExprSkip ;
// The header pieces after `object` - type parameters, a visibility modifier and a
// constructor parameter list - are all SEMANTIC errors on an object expression, never
// syntactic ones: psi/recovery/objects/expressions/Everything.txt records
// `object<T, R> private (x: Int, y: Int) : Bar, Baz { ... }` with no PsiErrorElement at
// all. They are parsed and dropped. The twin of ObjectExprReal in kotlin-interpreter.abnf.
ObjectExprReal <~~ push(makeObjectExpr(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwObject [ SkipAngle ] [ ObjVis ] [ CtorParamsSkip ] [ ":" Supers ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
ObjectExprSkip <~~ push(notImplExpr("object expression", up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwObject [ SkipAngle ] [ ObjVis ] [ CtorParamsSkip ] [ ":" SuperList ] SkipBlock ;
ObjVis      = ( "private" | "protected" | "internal" | "public" ) KwEnd ;

// A labelled receiver - this@Outer. When the label names the class whose members are
// being emitted it is just `this`; when it names an ENCLOSING class it is the inner
// instance's stored outer receiver (__outer), which is what an inner class needs. Bare
// `this` is a plain VarRef; this must precede Call/VarRef so `this` is not read as an
// identifier before the `@Label` is seen.
ThisExpr    <~~ push(makeThisAt(pop().tlbl)) ~~>
            = "this" KwEnd "@" KId <~~ push({tlbl: up.in}) ~~> ;

// A lambda: { it * 2 }, { x -> x * 2 }, { a: Int, b: Int -> a + b }. The body is
// one expression; without parameters the single argument is bound to 'it'.
// A lambda may carry its own LABEL - exec lab@{ ... return@lab } - which becomes the
// target of a return@lab inside it (instead of the callee's implicit label). LamLabel
// registers it on the walk-time return-target stack; makeLambda pops it again.
Lambda      <~~ ktNoteBody(up.start, up.pos); push(makeLambda(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LamLabel ] "{" [ LambdaParams ] { LamStmt } "}" ;
LamLabel    = KId <~~ push({lamlbl: up.in}); retLblPush(up.in, "lam") ~~> AtAdjacent "@" ;
// A { ... } in value position (an if / when arm) is a BLOCK, not a lambda: it runs its
// statements in a fresh scope and its value is the last expression (Unit if it ends in a
// statement). Tried before a plain Expression in those arms; a real lambda ({ x -> ... })
// has parameters, so it fails ValueBlock and falls through to Lambda.
ValueBlock  <~~ push(makeValueBlock(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" { LamStmt } "}" ;
// A lambda body is a sequence of statements whose value is its last expression
// (a single expression is the common case). Genuinely lowered, not notImpl.
// A leading annotation (runCatching { @Suppress("DEPRECATION") val x = ... }) is
// parsed and ignored, exactly as at the top statement level; keyword-led statements
// first; then an if/else whose arms are expressions (an IfExpr keeps its value); then
// any REMAINING if statement - a bare `if (c) s` must be consumed here, BEFORE LamExpr,
// which would otherwise read the keyword `if` as an identifier (`if (c)` parses as the
// call if(c)) and only fail at run time; then any expression; then an empty statement.
LamStmt     = { Annotation } ( LamCtrl | LamIfValue | LamIf | LamExpr ) | ";" ;
LamCtrl     <~~ push({stmt: pop()}) ~~>
            = ReturnAt | Return | BreakAt | Break | ContinueAt | Continue
            | Labeled | LocalFun | LocalModDecl | LocalType | Destructure | DelegateDecl
            | Decl | BareDecl | While | Do | For | TryStmt | Throw ;
LamIfValue  <~~ push({expr: pop()}) ~~>
            = IfExpr ;
LamIf       <~~ push({stmt: pop()}) ~~>
            = If ;
LamExpr     <~~ push({expr: pop()}) ~~>
            = Expression [ ";" ] ;
// A parameter may be ABSENT: `{ -> 1 }` is a lambda with an empty parameter list and
// `{ a, , b -> }` is what Kotlin's parser builds for a hole (psi/greatSyntacticShift/
// functionLiterals.txt records both with a clean tree). Same spelling as the interpreter.
LambdaParams <~~ push({lparams: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = [ LParam ] { "," [ LParam ] } "->" ;
// A lambda parameter is a plain name or a destructuring pattern - { (a, b) -> ... }
// over a Pair/data-class/Triple argument, whose names makeLambda binds through the
// componentN convention.
LParam      = DestructLParam | KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" Type ] ;
DestructLParam <~~ push({destruct: takeAll(), pos: up.pos}) ~~>   // Each entry pushes {dn, dprop}.
            = DPat [ ":" Type ] ;

// f(a, b): a top level function, a builtin, or a class constructor. A trailing lambda
// becomes the last argument (Kotlin): after a parenthesized list - f(a) { ... } - or on
// its own - f { ... } (the sole argument, e.g. module { single { ... } }). Either form
// works as a Primary anywhere, including as an argument to an outer call
// (modules(module(createdAtStart = true) { ... }, module { ... }, ...)).
Call        <~~ push(makeCall(takeAll())); retLblPop() ~~>
            = NotHardKw KId <~~ push(up.in); retLblPush(up.in, "lam") ~~> [ TypeArgs ] ( SameLine "(" [ CallArgs ] ")" { TrailLambda } | TrailLambda { TrailLambda } ) ;
// Explicit type arguments at a call site. Erased, except when the callee has a reified
// type parameter: then the argument's NAME is what fills the callee's hidden parameter.
TypeArgs    <~~ push({targs: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = SkipAngle ;

// String templates: "x=$x sum=${a + b}".
Template    <~~ push(makeTemplate(takeAll())) ~~>
            = '"' :whitespace() { TplExpr | TplVar | TplDollar | TplChunk } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
TplExpr     <~~ push({e: pop()}) ~~>
            = "${" :whitespace(Whitespace) Expression "}" ;
// A SHORT template entry names a variable, and a hard keyword is not one: Kotlin
// records `"$try"` as an error (psi/stringTemplates/
// StringTemplateWithTryWithoutBlockInShortEntry.kt), and the long form `${try ...}` is
// how a keyword-led expression is interpolated. `$` on its own is already ordinary text
// in TplPlain, so a refused TplVar cannot fall through to TplChunk and the whole string
// is refused - which is the reject this guard is for. Same spelling as the interpreter.
TplVar      = "$" NotHardKw TplName <~~ push({e: makeVarRef(up.in)}) ~~> ;
TplName     = ( "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "_" ) { "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "0"..."9" | "_" } ;
// A lone `$` - `"$"`, `"cost: 100$"`, `"$.$.asdf"` - is ordinary string text in Kotlin.
// Twin of TplDollar in kotlin-interpreter.abnf; see there.
TplDollar   <~~ push({s: "$"}) ~~>
            = "$" NotTplStart ;
NotTplStart = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var c0 = c.peek(0)
        if ((c0 >= 97 && c0 <= 122) || (c0 >= 65 && c0 <= 90) || c0 === 95 || c0 === 123) {
            return abnf.newToken("\x01tplstart", 0)
        }
    })()
~~) ;
TplChunk    <~~ push({s: unescapeJs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = TplChar { TplChar } ;
TplChar     = TplEsc | TplPlain ;
TplEsc      = "\\" "\x20"..b"\x7e" ;
// Everything but the closing quote, the escape backslash, the template `$` and a raw
// newline. Kotlin allows the other control characters (a vertical tab, a form feed, a
// NEL) inside a "..." literal, so the low range is included except \n and \r.
TplPlain    = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b"..."\x0c" | "\x0e"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x23"
            | "\x25"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;

Null        <~~ push(makeConst(null)) ~~>  = "null" KwEnd ;

VarRef      <~~ push(makeVarRef(up.in)) ~~>
            = NotHardKw KId ;

// A zero-width guard that fails when the next word is one of Kotlin's HARD keywords -
// the ones that can never be a name. Without it a bare `if`, `for`, `while`, `when` or
// `val` would parse as a variable REFERENCE and a `for ()` / `if ()` as a CALL, and the
// psi/recovery corpus (deliberately truncated control structures, each one a syntax
// error) would be accepted. It guards the two expression rules that read a bare name,
// VarRef and Call; the soft keywords (get/set/data/value/by/where/companion/field/...)
// are deliberately NOT listed, because Kotlin really does allow them as names and the
// psi corpus tests exactly that. A backtick-quoted name scans as the empty word and is
// allowed: `if` IS a legal identifier. `this` is deliberately absent as well: a BARE
// `this` (no @label) is lowered through VarRef, which is where the receiver lives.
NotHardKw   = :script(~~
    (function() {
        // catch / finally are here for the same reason the interpreter half has them:
        // `catch` with no parameter list (psi/recovery/CatchKeywordRBrace.kt) must not
        // parse as an ordinary identifier expression.
        var hard = "|as|break|catch|class|continue|do|else|false|finally|for|fun|if|in"
            + "|interface|is|null"
            + "|object|package|return|super|throw|true|try|typealias|typeof|val|var"
            + "|when|while|"
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 10 || cc === 13) { i++; cc = c.peek(i) }
        var w = ""
        while ((cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || cc === 95) {
            w = w + String.fromCharCode(cc)
            i++
            cc = c.peek(i)
        }
        return (w !== "" && hard.indexOf("|" + w + "|") >= 0)
            ? abnf.newToken("\x01hard-keyword-follows", 0)
            : undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// ----- Widened surface: constructs accepted structurally but not lowered -----

// A balanced { ... } body consumed without interpreting it, so a construct can be
// ACCEPTED without being understood. Full string/char literals and ${...} templates
// are matched first, so braces inside them are never miscounted. No tags: nothing is
// pushed, so the consuming production is free to push its own placeholder.
SkipBlock   = "{" { SkipItem } "}" ;
SkipItem    = SkipStr | SkipChar | SkipBlock | SkipOther ;
SkipStr     = '"' :whitespace() { SkipTpl | SkipStrEsc | SkipStrChar } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SkipTpl     = "${" :whitespace(Whitespace) { SkipItem } "}" ;
SkipStrEsc  = "\\" "\x00"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipStrChar = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipChar    = "'" :whitespace() { SkipChEsc | SkipChChar } "'" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SkipChEsc   = "\\" "\x00"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipChChar  = "\x00"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipOther   = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x7a" | "\x7c" | "\x7e"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// A balanced < ... > run (generic parameters / arguments), parsed and ignored. `->` is
// matched as one token, so a FUNCTION TYPE inside the run - Map<String, () -> Unit>,
// <T3 : (Int) -> Int> - does not end it at the `>` of its arrow.
SkipAngle   = "<" { SkipAngle | "->" | SkipAngleItem } ">" ;
SkipAngleItem = "\x00"..."\x3b" | "\x3d" | "\x3f"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// A balanced ( ... ) run (used to skip an anonymous function's parameter list, which
// may hold nested parens from function types). No tags: nothing is pushed.
SkipParen   = "(" { SkipParen | SkipParenItem } ")" ;
SkipParenItem = "\x00"..."\x27" | "\x2a"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// A statement label (loop@, outer@): the Labeled wrapper offers the name to
// the labelled loop's loopStack entry and gives labelled BLOCKS a break target
// (see makeLabeled). break@l / continue@l branch to the loop labelled l. return@l
// is genuine when l names the INNERMOST enclosing lambda's implicit label (the
// callee name: forEach in list.forEach { return@forEach v }) or the directly
// enclosing function - both compile to a plain return, because the innermost
// lambda IS the current IR function (makeReturn's retStmt stays try-aware). The
// walk-time retLabels stack decides (see makeReturnAt); a NON-LOCAL labelled
// return (an outer lambda's label) stays not implemented, as does a labelled
// break/continue out of a try body closure.
// The label NAME may not be a hard keyword: `fun @[a] T.() {}` in a block would read as
// the label `fun@` followed by the collection literal `[a]`, and the parse would die on
// the `.` of `T.()` two tokens later (psi/FunctionExpressions.kt).
// The `@` must be GLUED to the name; twin of LabelDef in kotlin-interpreter.abnf, see
// there.
LabelDef    = NotHardKw KId <~~ push({lbl: up.in}) ~~> AtAdjacent "@" ;
BreakAt     <~~ push(makeBreakTo(pop().lbl, up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwBreak AtAdjacent "@" KId <~~ push({lbl: up.in}) ~~> [ ";" ] ;
ContinueAt  <~~ push(makeContinueTo(pop().lbl, up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwContinue AtAdjacent "@" KId <~~ push({lbl: up.in}) ~~> [ ";" ] ;
ReturnAt    <~~ push(makeReturnAt(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwReturn AtAdjacent "@" KId <~~ push({rlbl: up.in}) ~~> [ SameLine Expression ] [ ";" ] ;
// A zero-width guard that fails unless the VERY NEXT character is `@`, with no
// whitespace in between. Kotlin's label reference is one token: `break@loop` is a
// labelled break, `break @loop` is a break followed by a dangling annotation and is
// rejected (psi/newLabels/spaceBeforeLabelReference). Written as a guard rather than
// with :whitespace() so that a failing alternative cannot leak the whitespace setting.
AtAdjacent  = :script(~~
    (function() {
        return (c.peek(0) === 64) ? undefined : abnf.newToken("\x01need-adjacent-at", 0)
    })()
~~) ;
// The other half of the same rule: a `@` reachable from a control keyword across
// whitespace OR comments is an error, not an annotation. Twin of NoSpacedAt in
// kotlin-interpreter.abnf; see there.
NoSpacedAt  = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var steps = 0
        while (steps < 100000) {
            steps++
            var ch = c.peek(i)
            if (ch === 32 || ch === 9 || ch === 10 || ch === 13 || ch === 12) { i++ }
            else if (ch === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) {
                i = i + 2
                var d = c.peek(i)
                while (d >= 0 && d !== 10 && steps < 100000) { steps++; i++; d = c.peek(i) }
            }
            else if (ch === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 42) {
                i = i + 2
                var e = c.peek(i)
                while (e >= 0 && steps < 100000) {
                    steps++
                    if (e === 42 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) { i = i + 2; break }
                    i++
                    e = c.peek(i)
                }
            }
            else { break }
        }
        if (c.peek(i) === 64) { return abnf.newToken("\x01spaced-label-ref", 0) }
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// A floating-point literal (1.5, 2.0f, 1e9, 3.14E-2, digit-group underscores):
// a genuine double value (parseFloat stops before the f/F suffix). Comparisons
// are exact; see the arithmetic note at the core config for the int-wrap caveat.
FloatLit    <~~ push(makeFloConst(parseFloat(up.in.split("_").join("")), ktIsF32Lit(up.in))) ~~>
            = ( "0" | "1"..."9" ) :whitespace() { "0"..."9" | "_" }
              ( "." "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" | "_" } [ FloatExp ] [ FloatSfx ]
              | FloatExp [ FloatSfx ]
              | FloatSfx ) :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
FloatExp    = ( "e" | "E" ) [ "+" | "-" ] "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" | "_" } ;
// A float literal written without its integer part (.5, .1_1). Only reachable at the
// start of an operand, where a lone `.` cannot mean anything else. (psi/FloatingPointLiteral.kt)
DotFloatLit <~~ push(makeFloConst(parseFloat("0" + up.in.split("_").join("")), ktIsF32Lit(up.in))) ~~>
            = "." :whitespace() "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" | "_" } [ FloatExp ] [ FloatSfx ] :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
FloatSfx    = "f" | "F" ;

// Integer literal forms beyond the plain decimal IntLit: hex, binary, digit-group
// underscores and the L / u / U / uL / UL suffixes. Each tag DROPS the constant the
// shared NumInt / NumHex / NumBin pushed (a JS double, which already rounds
// 9223372036854775807) and rebuilds the literal from its own text, so the digits
// reach makeIntConst exactly.
KHexLit     <~~ pop(); push(makeIntConst(up.in)) ~~> = NumHex [ IntSfx ] ;
KBinLit     <~~ pop(); push(makeIntConst(up.in)) ~~> = NumBin [ IntSfx ] ;
KIntLit     <~~ pop(); push(makeIntConst(up.in)) ~~> = NumInt [ IntSfx ] ;
// Kotlin's integer literal suffixes: L (Long), u/U (unsigned) and the uL/UL pair.
// The trailing KwEnd is essential: the literal's own whitespace is consumed before the
// suffix is tried, so without a word boundary `0 until n` would read as `0u` followed
// by the method call `ntil`.
// Kotlin's own psi/IntegerLiteral.kt spells `1l`, `0x1l` and `0X1l` with a clean tree,
// so the lower-case `l` is part of the language here too (the interpreter half accepts it).
IntSfx      = ( ( "u" | "U" ) [ "l" | "L" ] | "l" | "L" ) KwEnd ;

// A character literal is a genuine Char value (the js_char extern boxes the code):
// it RENDERS as its glyph in a string or in println, but COMPARES and does ARITHMETIC
// on its code, so 'A' + 1 == 'B', 'z' > 'a' and "" + 'b' == "b" all hold. The
// kotlin-interpreter grammar boxes the same value as {__char: code}; the two agree.
CharLit     <~~ push(makeCharConst(kCharCode(up.in))) ~~>
            = "'" :whitespace() CharBody "'" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
CharBody    = "\\" ( "u" NumHexDig NumHexDig NumHexDig NumHexDig | CharEsc ) | CharPlain ;
CharEsc     = "n" | "t" | "r" | "b" | "'" | '"' | "\\" | "$" | "0" ;
// Everything but the closing quote, the escape backslash and a line break. Kotlin
// allows the other control characters (a vertical tab, a form feed) in a char literal,
// so the low range is included except \n and \r - the same rule TplPlain follows.
CharPlain   = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b"..."\x0c" | "\x0e"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// A triple-quoted raw string: content up to the closing """ (internal runs of one
// or two quotes are allowed), kept verbatim - no escape processing - with genuine
// $name / ${expr} interpolation (same maker as Template). A $ directly after an
// internal quote is consumed as raw text (exotic corner, kept simple).
RawStr      <~~ push(makeTemplate(takeAll())) ~~>
            = '"""' :whitespace() { TplExpr | TplVar | TplDollar | RawChunk } RawClose :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// A raw string ends at the LAST """ of a run of quotes, so the surplus quotes belong to
// the CONTENT (""""""" is the one-character string "). The twin of RawClose in
// kotlin-interpreter.abnf - without it the two halves disagree on the string's value.
RawClose    = RawCloseQ RawClose | '"""' ;
RawCloseQ   <~~ push({s: up.in}) ~~> = '"' ;
RawChunk    <~~ push({s: up.in}) ~~>
            = ( RawTwoQ | RawOneQ | RawPlain ) { RawTwoQ | RawOneQ | RawPlain } ;
RawPlain    = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x23" | "\x25"..."\U0010ffff" ;
RawOneQ     = '"' ( "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\U0010ffff" ) ;
RawTwoQ     = '"' '"' ( "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\U0010ffff" ) ;

// A callable reference. A bare ::fn is genuine: top-level functions and builtins are
// already first-class closures, so ::fn lowers exactly like the name itself (stored,
// passed as a lambda, called later); ::Cls is a CONSTRUCTOR reference and js_ktbareref
// turns the descriptor into one. A QUALIFIED reference (Type::member, recv::method,
// recv::prop) becomes a runtime reference value - see makeQualRef and the
// js_ktref / js_ktclasslit block in abnf/jsrtkotlin.go. Every form here is the twin of
// one in kotlin-interpreter.abnf; ./test.sh --cross diffs the two.
CallableRef = IsInitRef | BareClassRef | BareRef | QualRef ;
// A RECEIVERLESS class literal, `::class` (psi/DoubleColon.kt, clean tree). It names the
// class of the implicit receiver, which this subset does not model, so it is recognised
// and reported rather than guessed at. It must precede BareRef, whose KId cannot match
// the `class` keyword anyway - the ordering is what keeps the two apart if KId widens.
BareClassRef <~~ push(notImplExpr("receiverless class literal", up.pos)) ~~>
            = "::" "class" KwEnd ;
// `this::prop.isInitialized` - Kotlin's lateinit check. A `lateinit var` has no
// initializer, and a bodiless property defaults to null here, so "initialized" is
// exactly "the field is not null".
// The `this` is OPTIONAL: a bare `::prop.isInitialized` inside a member means the
// same thing (BareRef would READ the property and ask the null value for
// `.isInitialized`).
// The receiver is refused when it is anything OTHER than `this` / `this@Label` /
// absent: the Kotlin documentation ("Checking whether a lateinit var is initialized")
// makes the check available only for a property lexically accessible at the site, and a
// type-qualified `Type::prop` is a KProperty1 - an unbound reference - where
// isInitialized is declared on KProperty0 alone. `l::a.isInitialized` from OUTSIDE the
// declaring class is therefore a compile error, not `false`. The base is a full one so
// that the refusal happens HERE rather than falling through to QualRef, which would
// build a perfectly good bound reference and read a member off it.
IsInitRef   <~~ push(makeIsInit(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = [ QualRefBase [ "?" ] [ "?" ] ] "::" KId <~~ push({irname: up.in}) ~~> "." "isInitialized" KwEnd ;
BareRef     = "::" KId <~~ push(makeBareRef(up.in)) ~~> ;
// A NULLABLE receiver is allowed in front of the `::` - `X?::x`, `X ?:: x`, `X? ?:: x`,
// `X ??:: x`, `X ?? :: x`. All five are CALLABLE_REFERENCE_EXPRESSIONs in Kotlin's own
// tree (psi/greatSyntacticShift/nullableTypes.txt), differing only in where the QUEST
// tokens sit, and none of them is a syntax error. The `?`s belong to the receiver TYPE,
// which this form drops anyway, so they are consumed and ignored.
// This has to be matched at the Primary level, before Elvis gets to see the `?:` of
// `a?::b` as its own operator.
QualRef     <~~ var qn = pop(); push(makeQualRef(pop(), qn)) ~~>
// Each segment may carry TYPE ARGUMENTS - `a<b>?::c`, `a<b?,c?>?::d`, `a.b<c.d<e.f>>::g`
// and `a<b,c>::class` are all clean CALLABLE_REFERENCE_EXPRESSIONs in psi/DoubleColon.txt -
// and the referenced name may be `class` (a class literal). Both as in the interpreter half.
// The base may also carry a LABEL - `super<a>@b::foo` (psi/DoubleColon.txt records it as
// one CALLABLE_REFERENCE_EXPRESSION over a labelled super). The `::` that has to follow is
// what keeps this apart from an ordinary `label@ statement`.
            = QualRefBase [ "?" ] [ "?" ] "::" RefName ;
QualRefBase <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~>
            = KId [ SkipAngle ] [ "@" KId ] { "." KId [ SkipAngle ] } ;

// An anonymous function expression - fun(params): T { ... } or fun(params) = expr - is
// genuine: it lowers to exactly what a named local function lowers to (an own IR
// function plus a js_closure over the current scope), only without a name. Its `return`
// returns from IT, which is the whole point of the form (unlike a lambda's).
AnonFun     <~~ ktNoteBody(up.start, up.pos); push(makeAnonFun(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ ContextClause ] KwFun ParamList [ ":" Type ] [ ContractClause ] [ WhereClause ] ( Block | "=" ExprBody ) ;
// The anonymous-function shapes the subset cannot lower: one with a RECEIVER
// (fun T.(a: foo): bar {}), a NAMED one (fun name() = a), a generic one, and any of
// them with NO body at all - psi/FunctionExpressions.kt and psi/contextParameters/
// anonymousFunction.kt spell all of them out with clean trees. Reported, not lowered.
// Deliberately built from the REAL ParamList and FunBody rather than from SkipParen /
// SkipBlock: this alternative must be reachable only from a shape the strict AnonFun
// refused STRUCTURALLY, never from one whose body does not parse (see docs/abnf-dialect-
// gotchas.md, "A SKIPPED body makes a widening cost the reject corpus, invisibly").
// takeAll() drops whatever the parts pushed; the production yields one expression.
AnonFunUnsup <~~ takeAll(); push(notImplExpr("anonymous function", up.pos)) ~~>
            = [ ContextClause ] KwFun { TypeAnno } [ SkipAngle ] [ RecvSeg { RecvSeg } ] [ KId ] [ SkipAngle ]
              ParamList [ ":" Type ] [ ContractClause ] [ WhereClause ] [ FunBody ] ;

// A tag-less primary-constructor parameter list for headers we only skip.
CtorParamsSkip = "(" [ CParamSkip { "," CParamSkip } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
CParamSkip  = [ Modifiers ] [ KwVararg ] [ ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd ] KId ":" Type [ "=" DiscardExpr ] ;

// A supertype list (types with optional constructor-call args). No inheritance in
// the subset, so it is parsed and ignored; DiscardExpr drops the arg thunks.
SuperList   = SuperSkipItem { "," SuperSkipItem } ;
// A supertype is a class (optional constructor-call args), an interface, or an
// interface implemented by delegation (`: I by impl`). No inheritance in the
// subset, so all three parse and are ignored; the delegate expression is discarded.
SuperSkipItem = Type [ "(" [ DiscardExpr { "," DiscardExpr } [ "," ] ] ")" | KwBy DiscardExpr ] ;
DiscardExpr <~~ pop() ~~> = Expression ;

// Type declarations accepted structurally, then reported as not implemented (so a
// full Kotlin file parses; -warn-unsupported lets the rest compile). Body is skipped.
UnsupType   = Interface | ObjectDecl | EnumClass | AnnotationClass | TypeAlias ;
// An interface is lowered to a plain class descriptor: __isclass / __name / __super
// plus the closures of its DEFAULT methods. It has no __ctor (it is never
// constructed); its abstract members contribute only their names (see AbstractFun),
// and its properties are abstract, so no field is emitted - the implementing class
// initializes them on the instance, where js_ktget finds them. An implementing class
// puts the interface on its __super chain, so the runtime's memberCall finds the
// default methods and js_is_type answers `x is Interface`. The body is optional (a
// marker interface); a body outside the member subset falls back to InterfaceSkip.
Interface   = InterfaceCtor | InterfaceReal | InterfaceSkip ;
// interface I(val a: Int): an interface may not have a primary constructor either,
// and again Kotlin parses the list before rejecting it. Tried FIRST, because the
// bodiless InterfaceReal would otherwise match `interface I` and strand the `(`.
InterfaceCtor <~~ push(notImplStmt("interface", up.pos)) ~~>
            = [ KwFun ] KwInterface KId [ SkipAngle ] [ PrimaryCtorKw | CtorModsOnly ] CtorParamsSkip
              [ ":" SuperList ] [ SkipBlock ] ;
// `fun interface` is a functional (SAM) interface: with exactly ONE abstract member it
// admits SAM conversion, `F { ... }` building an instance whose single member IS the
// lambda (see makeCall). The `fun` is recorded as a leading marker item, the way
// ClassDecl records `data`/`value`, so makeInterfaceStmt can tell the two apart -
// an ordinary interface with one abstract member is NOT convertible, which is
// Kotlin's rule. The twin of IfaceKw/buildInterface in kotlin-interpreter.abnf.
InterfaceReal <~~ ktNoteBody(up.start, up.pos); ktNoteTypeName(up.in, "interface"); push(makeInterfaceStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ KwFun <~~ push({samfun: true}) ~~> ] KwInterface KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] [ ":" Supers ] [ WhereClause ] ( "{" { Member } "}" | !"{" ) ;
InterfaceSkip <~~ push(notImplStmt("interface", up.pos)) ~~>
            = [ KwFun ] KwInterface KId [ SkipAngle ] [ ":" SuperList ] [ SkipBlock ] ;
// object Name { ... } is a real singleton: its member body is lowered into a backing
// js_obj_new (properties initialized once at program start, methods reachable via the
// name and dispatched through js_mcall). A body outside the subset (e.g. a char
// literal), or no body at all (a bodiless object), falls back to ObjectSkip
// (notImplemented), so the widened surface still parses.
ObjectDecl  = ObjectCtor | ObjectReal | ObjectNoBody | ObjectSkip ;
ObjectReal  <~~ ktNoteBody(up.start, up.pos); ktNoteTypeName(up.in, "object"); push(makeObjectStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwObject KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" Supers ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
// object Foo(a: Int) / object Foo private () / object Foo<T, R>: an object declaration
// may not HAVE a primary constructor or type parameters, but that is a semantic rule -
// Kotlin's PARSER builds the shape and its recovery corpus records it without an error
// element (psi/recovery/objects/declarations/TypeParameterss.kt). At least one of the
// two must be present, so an ordinary `object Foo` still reaches ObjectReal - which is
// why the angle run cannot simply be made optional here (ObjectNoBody would then
// swallow `object Foo` and strand the `<T, R>`). Same shape as the interpreter half's
// ObjectExotic.
ObjectCtor  <~~ push(notImplStmt("object declaration", up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwObject KId ( SkipAngle [ [ PrimaryCtorKw | CtorModsOnly ] CtorParamsSkip ]
                           | [ PrimaryCtorKw | CtorModsOnly ] CtorParamsSkip )
              [ ":" SuperList ] [ WhereClause ] [ SkipBlock ] ;
// A BODILESS object declaration (object Zero : Expr) is a genuine singleton too - the
// common shape of a sealed hierarchy's "no payload" case. The !"{" lookahead keeps it
// from stealing an object whose body does not parse; that one still falls to ObjectSkip.
ObjectNoBody <~~ ktNoteTypeName(up.in, "object"); push(makeObjectStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwObject KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" Supers ] !"{" ;
// An object declaration may not DECLARE type parameters, but Kotlin parses them and
// then reports the error semantically - psi/recovery/objects/declarations/TypeParameterss.kt
// records `object Foo<T, R> {}` with a clean tree. The angle run is skipped here (this
// alternative is already the notImplemented fallback), as the interpreter half does.
ObjectSkip  <~~ push(notImplStmt("object declaration", up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwObject KId [ SkipAngle ] [ ":" SuperList ] [ WhereClause ] [ SkipBlock ] ;
// enum class E(ctorParams) { A(args), B(args); members }: genuine. The descriptor is an
// ordinary class descriptor (so its methods dispatch and `x is E` works) and each entry
// is a singleton instance built once at program start, carrying the synthesized `name`
// and `ordinal` plus whatever the primary constructor set. The entries become both
// properties of the descriptor (E.A) and bindings of a scope the enum's own members
// close over, which is what makes the BARE entry names work inside them
// (when (this) { A -> ... }). An entry with its own body, and a body outside the member
// subset, fall back to EnumSkip.
EnumClass   = EnumReal | EnumSkip ;
EnumReal    <~~ ktNoteBody(up.start, up.pos); ktNoteTypeName(up.in, "class"); push(makeEnumStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwEnum KwClass KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] [ CtorParams ] [ ":" Supers ]
              "{" [ EnumEntry { "," EnumEntry } [ "," ] ] [ ";" { Member } ] "}" ;
// An entry may carry its OWN CLASS BODY - `LOW(1) { override fun label() = "low" }` -
// which is how an enum with an abstract member is written, and the shape any
// per-constant behaviour takes. Each such entry gets its own descriptor whose __super
// is the enum's, so the override wins and everything the enum itself declares is still
// inherited.
EnumEntry   <~~ push(makeEnumEntry(takeAll())) ~~>
            = { Annotation } KId <~~ push({ename: up.in}) ~~> [ "(" [ CallArgs ] ")" ] [ EnumEntryBody ] ;
EnumEntryBody <~~ push({emembers: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "{" { Member } "}" ;
// The fallback for an enum whose ENTRIES or MEMBERS are outside the subset. It skips
// the body's contents, but it keeps the body's SHAPE: a comma-separated entry list and
// then, only after a `;`, the members. A plain SkipBlock here would accept every one of
// the psi/EnumEntry* corpus files - `enum class My { FIRST, fun foo() {} }`, `FIRST,,`,
// `FIRST: My(13)` - which Kotlin rejects with "Expecting ';' after the last enum entry".
EnumSkip    <~~ push(notImplStmt("enum class", up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwEnum KwClass KId [ SkipAngle ] [ CtorParamsSkip ] [ ":" SuperList ] EnumSkipBody ;
// The member run after the `;` is tried as real members FIRST: an enum reaches EnumSkip
// because its ENTRIES are outside the subset, and its members are usually ordinary. A
// bare `{ SkipItem }` run would swallow `inner object` (psi/namelessObjectAsEnumMember.txt
// records "Name expected"). Two full alternatives, not a group - a group that matched
// the empty repetition would commit and never offer the second (gotchas.md, "A GROUP of
// alternatives is committed exactly like an ordered choice").
EnumSkipBody = "{" [ EnumEntrySkip { "," EnumEntrySkip } [ "," ] ] [ ";" { DropMember } ] "}" ;
DropMember  <~~ takeAll() ~~> = Member ;
// An entry may carry modifiers and annotations in any order (`private @Ann() Z()`,
// and `inline public` - where `inline` is a modifier and `public` the entry NAME).
EnumEntrySkip = { ( Modifier | Annotation ) NotEntryEnd } NotHardKw KId [ SkipParen ] [ SkipBlock ] ;
// A zero-width guard that fails when the next non-whitespace character ends an enum
// entry - `,` `;` `}` - or opens its arguments or body - `(` `{`. It is what keeps the
// modifier run from eating the entry's NAME when that name is itself a soft keyword:
// in `enum class My { inline public, ... }` the entry is named `public` and only
// `inline` is a modifier, and a PEG repetition never gives a consumed word back.
NotEntryEnd = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 10 || cc === 13) { i++; cc = c.peek(i) }
        return (cc === 44 || cc === 59 || cc === 125 || cc === 40 || cc === 123)
            ? abnf.newToken("\x01entry-end-follows", 0) : undefined
    })()
~~) ;
// An annotation class declares metadata only: annotations themselves are parsed and
// dropped everywhere, so the declaration has nothing to emit - it is ERASED, not
// unsupported. Same for a typealias: the alias names a type, and types are erased in
// the untyped handle world, so every use of the alias already compiles.
AnnotationClass <~~ push(makeErased()) ~~>
            = KwAnnotation KwClass KId [ SkipAngle ] [ CtorParamsSkip ] [ SkipBlock ] ;
// The declaration itself is still erased, but the alias -> target mapping is RECORDED:
// `typealias Alias = Real ; class C : Alias()` names its superclass through the alias,
// and resolvedTypeName has to be able to follow it for `super.g()` inside C. The
// interpreter half keeps the same table (typeAliases).
TypeAlias   <~~ push(registerTypeAlias(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwTypealias KId <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] "=" TypeName [ ";" ] ;

// An init block: genuine. Its statements run in the constructor, interleaved with the
// property initializers in DECLARATION order (Kotlin's rule), with `this` bound - so
// an init block can read the constructor parameters and write the properties declared
// above it. It pushes {initblk} and makeClassStmt keeps the source order.
InitBlock   <~~ push({initblk: pop()}) ~~>
            = KwInit Block ;
// companion object { ... } inside a class: its members are attached to the class
// descriptor (properties as fields, methods into a holder the descriptor's __class
// points at), so ClassName.member and ClassName.m() work. A body outside the subset
// falls back to CompanionSkip (notImplemented).
CompanionObject = CompanionBlock | CompanionReal ;
// `companion { ... }` - the brace-only spelling of a companion object. Same members,
// same lowering; it just has no `object` keyword and no name.
CompanionBlock <~~ push({companion: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = KwCompanion "{" { Member } "}" ;
CompanionReal <~~ push({companion: takeAll()}) ~~>
// The optional NAME must open on the `object` keyword's own line. `object` is a soft
// keyword and therefore a perfectly good KId, so a nameless `companion object` followed
// by a blank line and a nested `object C` (psi/NamedClassObject.kt) would have the NEXT
// declaration's `object` eaten as the companion's name.
// The supertype list is `Supers`, not the discarding `SuperList`: a companion object
// may IMPLEMENT an interface (`companion object : Named`), and its default methods have
// to be copied onto the companion's method holder for `Cls.show()`.
            = KwCompanion KwObject [ SameLine KId <~~ push({cname: up.in}) ~~> ] [ ":" Supers ] ( "{" { Member } "}" | !"{" ) ;
CompanionSkip <~~ notImpl("companion object", up.pos) ~~>
            = KwCompanion KwObject [ KId ] [ ":" SuperList ] SkipBlock ;
// A secondary constructor - constructor(a, b) : this(a + b) { ... } - is genuine. It
// becomes its own IR function that first runs the delegation (the primary constructor on
// the SAME instance, or the superclass constructor for `: super(...)`) and then its own
// body. The class's __ctor becomes a small dispatcher that picks the constructor whose
// parameter count matches the call, which is how the subset models constructor
// overloading. The body is optional (a purely delegating secondary constructor).
SecondaryCtor <~~ push({sctor: makeSecondaryCtor(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = KwConstructor ParamList [ ":" CtorDelegate ] [ Block ] ;
CtorDelegate <~~ push({cdeleg: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = ( "this" | "super" ) <~~ push({dkind: up.in}) ~~> KwEnd "(" [ CallArgs ] ")" ;
// A property with ACCESSORS - val/var name [: T] [= init] get() ... set(v) ... - is
// genuine inside a class, an object or a companion. The pair becomes a js_defprop
// accessor on the class DESCRIPTOR, and the runtime resolves it along the same
// __class/__super chain it walks for methods, so plain `obj.name` reads and
// `obj.name = v` writes run the accessor bodies without the call sites knowing.
// The backing field lives on the INSTANCE under "name$f" - a descriptor accessor is
// only consulted when the instance has no own property of that name, so the backing
// field must NOT be called `name` - and `field` inside an accessor body is exactly that
// slot (see the makeVarRef / makeAssign overrides). A property with only a getter and no
// initializer has no backing field, which is the computed-property case.
AccessorProp <~~ push({accprop: makeAccessorProp(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd KId <~~ push({apname: up.in}) ~~> [ ":" PType ] [ "=" PropInit ] AccRun ;
PropInit    <~~ push({apinit: pop()}) ~~>
            = Expression ;
Accessor    = GetAcc | SetAcc ;
// Kotlin allows at MOST one getter and one setter, in either order
// (psi/DuplicateAccessor.kt, `get() = 0 get() = 0`, is an error) - the way the
// interpreter half spells it.
AccRun      = GetAcc [ SetAcc ] | SetAcc [ GetAcc ] ;
GetAcc      <~~ push({getter: pop()}) ~~>
            = [ Modifiers ] KwGet "(" ")" [ ":" Type ] [ ContractClause ] AccBody [ ";" ] ;
SetAcc      <~~ push(makeSetAcc(takeAll())) ~~>
// The setter parameter may carry annotations (`@f @g set(@h v) {}`,
// psi/annotation/targeted/onGetSetSparam/twoAnnotationLists.kt) and a trailing comma
// (`set(value,) { }`, psi/trailingCommaAllowed.kt) - both as in the interpreter half.
            = [ Modifiers ] KwSet "(" [ { Annotation } KId <~~ push({sparam: up.in}) ~~> [ ":" Type ] ] [ "," ] ")" [ ":" Type ] [ ContractClause ] AccBody [ ";" ] ;
AccBody     = Block | "=" ExprBody ;
// An EXTENSION property - val String.mid: Char get() = this[length / 2] - is genuine
// too, and lowers exactly like an extension function: its getter becomes an IR function
// with the receiver in the instance slot, declared as "extp$name", and the field-access
// suffix emitter routes recv.name to it (dispatch is by NAME, see extPropResolves).
// An extension property may carry an INITIALIZER as well as its accessors -
// `val Int.value : T = foo.bar() get() = data[this] set(it) { field = it }`, which
// psi/examples/priorityqueues/BinaryHeap.txt records with a clean tree. Kotlin rejects
// it SEMANTICALLY (an extension property has no backing field), not syntactically, and
// the initializer is dropped here for the same reason.
ExtProp     <~~ push(makeExtProp(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd [ SkipAngle ] { TypeAnno } RecvSeg { RecvSeg } KId <~~ push({apname: up.in}) ~~> [ ":" Type ] [ WhereClause ] [ "=" DiscardExpr ] AccRun ;
// An EXTENSION DELEGATED property - `val Ctx.store: Int by D()`, the shape the Android
// DataStore idiom (`val Context.dataStore by preferencesDataStore(name = "settings")`)
// is written in. GENUINE, and the twin of ExtDelegProp in kotlin-interpreter.abnf: the
// delegate belongs to the DECLARATION SITE, so the `by` expression is lowered once, at
// the declaration, and both accessors close over the resulting box; every read of
// `recv.name` calls getValue(recv, property) with the EXTENSION RECEIVER as thisRef and
// every write setValue(recv, property, value). The accessors are ordinary extension
// accessors (extp$name / extps$name), so the field-read and field-assign emitters route
// to them by NAME exactly as they do for a computed extension property.
// Spec-cited (no kotlinc here): the operator arity and the thisRef identity are read off
// the Kotlin documentation's "Delegated properties" page.
ExtDelegProp <~~ push(makeExtDelegProp(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd [ SkipAngle ] { TypeAnno } RecvSeg { RecvSeg } KId <~~ push({apname: up.in}) ~~> [ ":" Type ] [ WhereClause ] KwBy DelegSrc { PropVis } [ ";" ] ;
// A MEMBER extension PROPERTY - `val Int.scaled: Int get() = this * factor` declared
// inside a class. The twin of MemberExtProp in kotlin-interpreter.abnf, and the property
// counterpart of MemberExtFun above: the accessor pair is installed on the descriptor as
// extget$name / extset$name and takes (dispatchReceiver, extensionReceiver[, value]), so
// `this` is the extension receiver and the enclosing instance rides along as __dispatch.
// Ordered before AccessorProp for the same reason the interpreter orders it before
// PropDecl: the receiver segment would otherwise be half-consumed as the property name.
MemberExtProp <~~ push({memberExtProp: makeAccessorProp(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd [ SkipAngle ] { TypeAnno } RecvSeg { RecvSeg } KId <~~ push({apname: up.in}) ~~> [ ":" Type ] [ WhereClause ] [ "=" DiscardExpr ] AccRun ;
// The member-level EXTENSION DELEGATED property - `val Ctx.mem: Int by D()` inside a
// class. Same protocol as the top-level ExtDelegProp (one delegate per declaration
// site, thisRef is the extension receiver) lowered onto the descriptor as the ordinary
// member-extension accessor pair. Ordered before AccessorProp / PropDelegate, neither
// of which has a receiver segment, for the reason MemberExtProp above is.
MemberExtDelegProp <~~ push({memberExtDeleg: makeDelegProp(takeAll(), up.pos)}) ~~>
            = ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd [ SkipAngle ] { TypeAnno } RecvSeg { RecvSeg } KId <~~ push({apname: up.in}) ~~> [ ":" Type ] [ WhereClause ] KwBy DelegSrc [ ";" ] { DropAcc } { PropVis } [ ";" ] ;
// A TOP-LEVEL computed property. There is no instance to hang an accessor on, so the
// getter runs ONCE at the declaration and the name is bound to that value - a deviation
// from Kotlin, which re-evaluates the getter on every read.
TopAccProp  <~~ push(makeTopAccProp(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd [ SkipAngle ] KId <~~ push({apname: up.in}) ~~> [ ":" Type ] [ WhereClause ] AccRun ;
// The forms left over: a delegated accessor property and anything else AccessorProp,
// ExtProp and the member-extension forms above did not take.
UnsupAccessor <~~ push(notImplStmt("property accessor", up.pos)) ~~>
            = ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd [ SkipAngle ] [ { TypeAnno } RecvSeg { RecvSeg } ] KId [ ":" Type ] [ WhereClause ] [ "=" DiscardExpr ] AccRun ;

// val (a, b) = e: genuine. Kotlin defines it through the componentN operator
// convention - component1() for the first name, component2() for the second - which is
// what a data class synthesizes and what any class can declare by hand. A name of `_`
// discards its component and its componentN() is not called, as in Kotlin.
Destructure = DestructKw | DestructPat ;
DestructKw  <~~ push(makeDestructure(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = ( "val" | "var" ) KwEnd DPat [ ":" Type ] "=" Expression [ ";" ] ;
// The keyword-less form, where the entries carry their own val/var: (val a, var b) = x
// and [val a, val b] = x. See DPatLead for why the parenthesized case demands one.
DestructPat <~~ push(makeDestructure(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = DPatLead [ ":" Type ] "=" Expression [ ";" ] ;

// Kotlin's identifiers, INCLUDING the backtick-quoted form: `top level`, `class`,
// `is`. Any character but a backtick and a line break may appear between the quotes,
// which is how a name that is a keyword, or that contains spaces, is written (test
// names, and names coming from another JVM language). The quotes stay part of the
// identifier TEXT here - a declaration and every use spell the name the same way, so
// they still resolve to each other, and no name can collide with a plain identifier.
// The shared lib/ident.abnf owns the unquoted form; KId is the Kotlin-level token that
// every production in this grammar refers to.
KId         = BQId | Id ;
BQId        = "`" :whitespace() BQChar { BQChar } "`" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
BQChar      = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b"..."\x0c" | "\x0e"..."\x5f" | "\x61"..."\U0010ffff" ;

KwLazy      = "lazy" KwEnd ;
KwInterface = "interface" KwEnd ;
KwObject    = "object" KwEnd ;
KwEnum      = "enum" KwEnd ;
KwAnnotation = "annotation" KwEnd ;
KwCompanion = "companion" KwEnd ;
KwTypealias = "typealias" KwEnd ;
KwConstructor = "constructor" KwEnd ;
KwInit      = "init" KwEnd ;
KwGet       = "get" KwEnd ;
KwSet       = "set" KwEnd ;

KwFun       = "fun" KwEnd ;
KwImport    = "import" KwEnd ;
KwPackage   = "package" KwEnd ;
KwAs        = "as" KwEnd ;
KwAsSafe    = "as?" ;
KwTry       = "try" KwEnd ;
KwCatch     = "catch" KwEnd ;
KwFinally   = "finally" KwEnd ;
KwThrow     = "throw" KwEnd ;
KwIs        = "is" KwEnd ;
KwNotIs     = "!is" KwEnd ;
