    // The sequence a `for` runs over. A MAP (the shared {__dict, keys, vals} handle) and
    // a SET ({__set, keys}) have no `length`, so the index loop below would run zero
    // times over either; Kotlin iterates a map's ENTRIES and a set's elements, and so
    // does the interpreter half (kIterable). One runtime call decides, so a value that
    // is neither a list, a string, a map nor a set keeps behaving as a list.
    // Each map entry carries key/value AND first/second, which is what makes both
    // `for (e in m)` and `for ((k, v) in m)` work (js_ktiter in abnf/jsrtkotlin.go).
